1
00:00:43,064 --> 00:00:44,021
[HORSES NEIGHING]

2
00:01:57,790 --> 00:01:58,748
Dismount!

3
00:02:05,755 --> 00:02:07,104
SCOUT:
Herman's horse-sick!

4
00:02:14,981 --> 00:02:16,592
SCOUTMASTER:
Chaps, no one wander off.

5
00:02:18,333 --> 00:02:20,552
Some of the passageways
in here can run for miles.

6
00:02:31,737 --> 00:02:33,522
HERMAN: I don't think
this is such a good idea.

7
00:02:34,914 --> 00:02:36,177
[DIGGING]

8
00:02:36,264 --> 00:02:37,090
[LAUGHTER]

9
00:02:38,309 --> 00:02:39,397
What is it?

10
00:02:55,674 --> 00:02:56,806
[DIGGING CONTINUES]

11
00:03:05,641 --> 00:03:07,077
FEDORA: Alfred,
did you get anything yet?

12
00:03:07,164 --> 00:03:08,426
Nothing. Then keep digging.

13
00:03:08,513 --> 00:03:10,602
The kid's got something!

14
00:03:10,689 --> 00:03:12,474
[WHOOPING]

15
00:03:12,561 --> 00:03:14,432
I got something, Garth!
I got something...

16
00:03:14,519 --> 00:03:15,781
I got something
right here.

17
00:03:15,868 --> 00:03:17,348
[WHOOPING]

18
00:03:22,353 --> 00:03:23,615
Oh, look at that!

19
00:03:25,617 --> 00:03:27,140
We're rich!
We're rich! Shut up. Shut up.

20
00:03:27,228 --> 00:03:28,707
Well, we're rich,
ain't we?

21
00:03:31,057 --> 00:03:32,058
Indy...

22
00:03:33,321 --> 00:03:34,191
Indy?

23
00:03:35,497 --> 00:03:37,325
What are they doing?

24
00:03:37,412 --> 00:03:39,762
Indiana? Indiana?

25
00:03:39,849 --> 00:03:40,632
[SHUSHING]

26
00:03:45,071 --> 00:03:47,248
HALF BREED:
Hey, we got to find
more stuff to bring back.

27
00:03:47,335 --> 00:03:48,640
It's the Cross of Coronado.

28
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:51,991
Cortez gave it to him
in 1520.

29
00:03:52,078 --> 00:03:54,864
MAN: Oh, boy!

30
00:03:54,951 --> 00:03:56,605
ROSCOE: I'm thinkin'
about raisin' my
dead mama, dig down

31
00:03:56,692 --> 00:03:59,564
and put it
on her bony finger!

32
00:03:59,651 --> 00:04:02,219
That cross is
an important artifact.
It belongs in a museum.

33
00:04:03,916 --> 00:04:05,831
Run back
and find the others.

34
00:04:05,918 --> 00:04:08,051
Tell Mr. Havelock
that there are men
looting in the caves.

35
00:04:08,138 --> 00:04:09,139
Have him bring
the sheriff. [HERMAN SHUDDERING]

36
00:04:09,226 --> 00:04:10,923
It's only a snake.

37
00:04:11,010 --> 00:04:12,403
Did you hear
what I said?

38
00:04:12,490 --> 00:04:14,405
Right. Run back...

39
00:04:14,492 --> 00:04:16,364
Mr. Havelock...
The sheriff...

40
00:04:16,451 --> 00:04:18,322
What, what are you
gonna do?

41
00:04:18,409 --> 00:04:20,150
I don't know.

42
00:04:20,237 --> 00:04:21,151
I'll think
of something.

43
00:04:27,853 --> 00:04:29,115
FEDORA:
Dig with your hands,
not with your mouth.

44
00:04:44,740 --> 00:04:46,089
He's got our thing!

45
00:04:46,176 --> 00:04:47,395
Get him! Get off me!

46
00:04:53,270 --> 00:04:55,098
Mr. Havelock! Anybody!

47
00:04:56,360 --> 00:04:58,275
Everybody's lost but me.

48
00:04:58,362 --> 00:05:00,103
There he is!
Let's go! Let's go!

49
00:05:04,586 --> 00:05:05,587
[WHISTLING]

50
00:05:17,468 --> 00:05:18,774
Hey! Hey, you! Dang it!

51
00:05:24,214 --> 00:05:25,171
Hey! Come back here!

52
00:05:25,258 --> 00:05:26,172
[WHISTLING]

53
00:05:39,621 --> 00:05:40,796
Come on! Get him!

54
00:05:49,718 --> 00:05:50,762
Hey, come back here!

55
00:06:23,839 --> 00:06:24,535
[GRUNTING]

56
00:06:25,362 --> 00:06:26,450
[GROWLING]

57
00:06:30,846 --> 00:06:31,716
[HISSING]

58
00:06:46,862 --> 00:06:47,993
[SHOUTING]

59
00:06:50,213 --> 00:06:51,519
[INDY SCREAMING]

60
00:06:57,525 --> 00:06:58,700
Here, let me.

61
00:07:19,634 --> 00:07:21,505
[ROARING]

62
00:07:36,999 --> 00:07:38,348
Holy smokes!

63
00:08:02,459 --> 00:08:03,547
Come on, kid.

64
00:08:06,507 --> 00:08:08,030
There's no way
out of this.

65
00:08:20,042 --> 00:08:21,043
[ROARS] Hey!

66
00:08:40,976 --> 00:08:42,238
Toss up the whip.

67
00:08:52,857 --> 00:08:55,294
You got heart, kid,
but that belongs to me.

68
00:08:55,381 --> 00:08:57,296
It belongs to Coronado.

69
00:08:57,383 --> 00:08:59,211
Coronado is dead,
and so are all of
his grandchildren.

70
00:08:59,298 --> 00:09:01,649
This should be
in a museum.

71
00:09:01,736 --> 00:09:02,998
Now give it back!

72
00:09:04,086 --> 00:09:04,826
[HISSING]

73
00:09:06,479 --> 00:09:08,830
A snake! Snake!

74
00:09:08,917 --> 00:09:10,353
FEDORA:
Don't let him get away!

75
00:09:12,050 --> 00:09:12,660
INDY: Magic?

76
00:09:14,966 --> 00:09:16,359
Hold it.

77
00:09:16,446 --> 00:09:17,665
Make sure
he doesn't double back.

78
00:09:34,725 --> 00:09:36,858
Okay, kid,
out of the box, now.

79
00:09:38,599 --> 00:09:39,600
Son of a...

80
00:09:51,524 --> 00:09:52,395
Damn.

81
00:10:03,928 --> 00:10:04,886
Dad!

82
00:10:07,279 --> 00:10:08,150
Dad!

83
00:10:10,021 --> 00:10:10,935
Dad.

84
00:10:12,633 --> 00:10:14,330
Dad... HENRY: Out.

85
00:10:14,417 --> 00:10:16,985
It's important. Then wait.
Count to 20.

86
00:10:17,072 --> 00:10:19,204
No, Dad.
You listen to me. Junior!

87
00:10:19,291 --> 00:10:20,075
One, two, three, four...

88
00:10:21,119 --> 00:10:21,946
HENRY: In Greek.

89
00:10:24,209 --> 00:10:25,950
[COUNTING IN GREEK]

90
00:10:26,037 --> 00:10:26,734
[BUGLE BLOWING]

91
00:10:35,830 --> 00:10:39,181
HENRY: "May he
who illuminated this,
illuminate me."

92
00:10:45,666 --> 00:10:47,145
I brought the sheriff.

93
00:10:49,104 --> 00:10:51,019
Just the man
I want to see.

94
00:10:51,106 --> 00:10:52,411
Now, there were
five or six of them...

95
00:10:52,498 --> 00:10:53,630
It's all right, son. They came after me...

96
00:10:53,717 --> 00:10:54,762
You still got it?

97
00:10:54,849 --> 00:10:56,024
Well, yes, sir.

98
00:10:56,111 --> 00:10:56,938
It's right here.

99
00:10:57,939 --> 00:10:59,331
I'm glad to see that

100
00:11:01,377 --> 00:11:03,466
because the rightful
owner of this cross

101
00:11:03,553 --> 00:11:04,772
won't press charges
if you give it back.

102
00:11:07,078 --> 00:11:09,037
He's got witnesses,
five or six of them.

103
00:11:20,004 --> 00:11:21,310
[WHOOPING] Yeah!

104
00:11:33,017 --> 00:11:33,931
Good day.

105
00:11:39,154 --> 00:11:40,459
You lost today, kid,

106
00:11:42,548 --> 00:11:44,420
but it doesn't mean
you have to like it.

107
00:12:11,447 --> 00:12:13,623
Small world, Dr. Jones.

108
00:12:13,710 --> 00:12:15,103
Too small for two of us.

109
00:12:24,721 --> 00:12:26,201
This is the second time
I've had to reclaim

110
00:12:26,288 --> 00:12:27,811
my property from you.

111
00:12:27,898 --> 00:12:29,378
That belongs
in a museum.

112
00:12:30,553 --> 00:12:31,815
So do you.

113
00:12:32,903 --> 00:12:34,426
Throw him over the side.

114
00:13:05,153 --> 00:13:06,589
[GRUNTING]

115
00:13:23,475 --> 00:13:24,215
Grab him, he's getting away!
Stop him!

116
00:14:19,705 --> 00:14:22,534
INDY: Archeology is
the search for fact...

117
00:14:25,624 --> 00:14:27,496
...not truth.

118
00:14:27,583 --> 00:14:29,193
If it's truth
you're interested in,

119
00:14:29,280 --> 00:14:33,241
Dr. Tyree's philosophy class
is right down the hall.

120
00:14:33,328 --> 00:14:36,157
So forget any ideas
you've got about lost
cities, exotic travel,

121
00:14:36,244 --> 00:14:37,854
and digging up
the world.

122
00:14:37,941 --> 00:14:39,812
We do not follow maps
to buried treasure,

123
00:14:39,900 --> 00:14:42,163
and "X" never, ever,
marks the spot.

124
00:14:46,645 --> 00:14:49,866
Seventy percent
of all archeology
is done in the library.

125
00:14:49,953 --> 00:14:51,172
Research. Reading.

126
00:14:54,697 --> 00:14:56,612
We cannot afford
to take mythology
at face value.

127
00:14:56,699 --> 00:14:57,569
[BELL RINGING]

128
00:14:59,267 --> 00:15:01,312
Next week, Egyptology,

129
00:15:01,399 --> 00:15:05,229
starting with
the excavation of Naukratis
by Flinders Petrie in 1885.

130
00:15:05,316 --> 00:15:07,797
I will be in my office
if anybody's got
any problems,

131
00:15:07,884 --> 00:15:09,320
for the next
hour and a half.

132
00:15:15,239 --> 00:15:16,893
Marcus, I did it.

133
00:15:17,676 --> 00:15:18,895
You've got it!

134
00:15:29,688 --> 00:15:31,952
You know how long I've
been looking for that?

135
00:15:32,039 --> 00:15:33,823
All your life.

136
00:15:33,910 --> 00:15:35,259
All my life.

137
00:15:35,346 --> 00:15:39,046
Well done, Indy.
Very well done, indeed.

138
00:15:39,133 --> 00:15:42,571
This will find
a place of honor in
our Spanish collection.

139
00:15:42,658 --> 00:15:46,140
We can discuss my
honorarium over dinner
and champagne tonight.

140
00:15:46,227 --> 00:15:47,663
Your treat. Yes.

141
00:15:49,143 --> 00:15:50,840
My treat.

142
00:15:50,927 --> 00:15:52,711
[STUDENTS CHATTERING]

143
00:15:53,625 --> 00:15:54,583
GIRL: Dr. Jones!

144
00:15:54,670 --> 00:15:55,845
Dr. Jones!

145
00:15:55,932 --> 00:15:57,412
[ALL TALKING TOGETHER]

146
00:16:01,198 --> 00:16:03,026
Shush! Shush!

147
00:16:03,113 --> 00:16:05,028
Dr. Jones,
I am so glad you're back.

148
00:16:05,115 --> 00:16:06,508
Your mail is on your desk.
Here are your phone messages.

149
00:16:06,595 --> 00:16:07,770
Dr. Jones,
you promised...

150
00:16:07,857 --> 00:16:09,119
This is your
appointment schedule,

151
00:16:09,206 --> 00:16:11,469
and these term papers
still haven't been graded.

152
00:16:11,556 --> 00:16:13,645
Okay. Irene,
put everybody's
name on a list,

153
00:16:13,732 --> 00:16:15,038
get the order
they arrived...

154
00:16:15,125 --> 00:16:17,084
Oh, come on, Dr. Jones. Dr. Jones.

155
00:16:17,171 --> 00:16:20,522
...and I'll see each and
every one of them in turn.

156
00:16:33,013 --> 00:16:34,144
"Venice, Italy."

157
00:16:35,058 --> 00:16:36,190
ALL: Dr. Jones!

158
00:17:03,826 --> 00:17:04,870
MAN: Dr. Jones?

159
00:17:11,181 --> 00:17:12,139
Dr. Jones.

160
00:17:29,025 --> 00:17:31,419
I trust your trip down
was comfortable, Dr. Jones.

161
00:17:32,985 --> 00:17:34,683
My men didn't
alarm you, I hope.

162
00:17:36,032 --> 00:17:38,382
My name is Donovan.
Walter Donovan.

163
00:17:38,469 --> 00:17:40,515
I know who you are,
Mr. Donovan.

164
00:17:40,602 --> 00:17:41,733
Your contributions
to the museum
over the years

165
00:17:41,820 --> 00:17:43,692
have been
extremely generous.

166
00:17:43,779 --> 00:17:46,173
Some of the pieces
in your collection here
are very impressive.

167
00:17:46,260 --> 00:17:49,828
Well, like yourself,
Dr. Jones, I have
a passion for antiquities.

168
00:17:49,915 --> 00:17:51,352
Have a look over here.

169
00:17:53,136 --> 00:17:54,703
This might interest you.

170
00:17:58,750 --> 00:18:01,144
INDY: Well, it's sandstone.
Christian symbol.

171
00:18:02,319 --> 00:18:04,582
Early Latin text.

172
00:18:04,669 --> 00:18:06,715
Mid-12th century,
I should think.

173
00:18:06,802 --> 00:18:08,282
That was our assessment,
as well.

174
00:18:08,369 --> 00:18:10,240
Where did this come from?

175
00:18:10,327 --> 00:18:12,721
My engineers unearthed it
in the mountain region
north of Ankara

176
00:18:12,808 --> 00:18:15,071
while excavating
for copper.

177
00:18:15,158 --> 00:18:16,942
Can you translate
the inscription?

178
00:18:18,596 --> 00:18:20,903
[READING LATIN]

179
00:18:20,990 --> 00:18:24,298
"...who drinks the water
I shall give him,
says the Lord,

180
00:18:24,385 --> 00:18:27,388
"will have a spring
inside him

181
00:18:27,475 --> 00:18:29,738
"welling up for
eternal life.

182
00:18:32,393 --> 00:18:34,090
"Let them bring me
to your holy mountain

183
00:18:34,177 --> 00:18:36,440
"in the place
where you dwell.

184
00:18:36,527 --> 00:18:38,703
"Across the desert
and through the mountain

185
00:18:40,357 --> 00:18:42,185
"to the Canyon of
the Crescent Moon,

186
00:18:43,795 --> 00:18:45,754
"to the temple
where the cup that...

187
00:18:50,150 --> 00:18:53,240
"Where the cup that holds
the blood of Jesus Christ
resides forever."

188
00:18:55,546 --> 00:18:57,418
The Holy Grail, Dr. Jones.

189
00:18:59,594 --> 00:19:01,987
The chalice used by Christ
during the Last Supper.

190
00:19:03,815 --> 00:19:06,775
The cup that
caught His blood
at the Crucifixion

191
00:19:06,862 --> 00:19:08,820
and was entrusted to
Joseph of Arimathea.

192
00:19:10,387 --> 00:19:12,389
The Arthur legend.

193
00:19:12,476 --> 00:19:14,696
I've heard this
bedtime story before.

194
00:19:14,783 --> 00:19:17,133
Eternal life, Dr. Jones!

195
00:19:17,220 --> 00:19:19,614
The gift of youth
to whoever drinks
from the Grail.

196
00:19:19,701 --> 00:19:21,790
Now, that's a bedtime story
I'd like to wake up to.

197
00:19:21,877 --> 00:19:23,922
An old man's dream.

198
00:19:24,009 --> 00:19:26,186
Every man's dream,
including your father's,
I believe.

199
00:19:30,755 --> 00:19:33,018
Grail lore is his hobby.

200
00:19:33,105 --> 00:19:34,759
He's a teacher of
medieval literature.

201
00:19:34,846 --> 00:19:35,978
The one the students hope
they don't get.

202
00:19:37,675 --> 00:19:40,069
Walter, you're
neglecting your guests.

203
00:19:40,156 --> 00:19:41,592
DONOVAN: Be along
in a moment, dear.

204
00:19:47,598 --> 00:19:49,905
Hard to resist,
isn't it?

205
00:19:49,992 --> 00:19:53,169
The Holy Grail's
final resting place
described in detail.

206
00:19:53,256 --> 00:19:55,519
What good is it?

207
00:19:55,606 --> 00:19:58,870
This Grail tablet
speaks of deserts
and mountains and canyons.

208
00:19:58,957 --> 00:20:00,568
It's pretty vague.

209
00:20:00,655 --> 00:20:02,657
Where do you want
to start looking?

210
00:20:02,744 --> 00:20:04,659
Maybe if the tablet
were intact, you'd have
something to go on,

211
00:20:04,746 --> 00:20:07,009
but the entire top
portion is missing.

212
00:20:07,096 --> 00:20:09,185
Just the same, an attempt
to recover the Grail is
currently underway.

213
00:20:12,971 --> 00:20:16,192
Let me tell you
another bedtime
story, Dr. Jones.

214
00:20:16,279 --> 00:20:19,413
After the Grail was
entrusted to Joseph
of Arimathea,

215
00:20:19,500 --> 00:20:22,416
it disappeared,
and was lost for
a thousand years

216
00:20:22,503 --> 00:20:25,593
before it was found again
by three knights of
the First Crusade.

217
00:20:25,680 --> 00:20:27,116
Three brothers,
to be exact.

218
00:20:27,203 --> 00:20:29,423
I've heard this one,
as well.

219
00:20:29,510 --> 00:20:31,468
Two of these brothers
walked out of the desert

220
00:20:31,555 --> 00:20:34,645
150 years after
having found the Grail

221
00:20:34,732 --> 00:20:36,604
and began the long journey
back to France,

222
00:20:36,691 --> 00:20:38,823
but only one of them
made it.

223
00:20:38,910 --> 00:20:42,305
And before dying
of extreme old age,

224
00:20:42,392 --> 00:20:45,003
he supposedly imparted
his tale to a...

225
00:20:45,090 --> 00:20:47,484
to a Franciscan friar,
I think.

226
00:20:47,571 --> 00:20:49,225
Not "supposedly,"
Dr. Jones.

227
00:20:50,270 --> 00:20:52,359
This is the manuscript

228
00:20:52,446 --> 00:20:55,840
in which the friar
chronicled
the knight's story.

229
00:20:55,927 --> 00:20:58,190
It doesn't reveal
the location of the Grail,
I'm afraid,

230
00:20:58,278 --> 00:20:59,844
but the knight promised
that two markers

231
00:20:59,931 --> 00:21:01,803
that had been
left behind would.

232
00:21:01,890 --> 00:21:05,241
This tablet is one
of those markers.

233
00:21:05,328 --> 00:21:07,765
It proves the knight's story
is true.

234
00:21:07,852 --> 00:21:10,464
But as you pointed out,
it's incomplete.

235
00:21:10,551 --> 00:21:13,989
Now, the second marker
is entombed with the
knight's dead brother.

236
00:21:14,076 --> 00:21:16,470
Our project leader
believes that tomb
to be located

237
00:21:16,557 --> 00:21:18,472
within the city
of Venice, Italy.

238
00:21:21,475 --> 00:21:23,999
As you can now see,
Dr. Jones,

239
00:21:24,086 --> 00:21:26,828
we're about to complete
a great quest

240
00:21:26,915 --> 00:21:29,178
that began almost
2,000 years ago.

241
00:21:30,440 --> 00:21:32,964
We're only
one step away.

242
00:21:33,051 --> 00:21:34,879
That's usually when
the ground falls out
from underneath your feet.

243
00:21:36,490 --> 00:21:37,578
You could be more
right than you know.

244
00:21:38,143 --> 00:21:39,623
Yes?

245
00:21:39,710 --> 00:21:40,885
We've hit a snag.

246
00:21:42,496 --> 00:21:45,107
Our project leader
has vanished,

247
00:21:45,194 --> 00:21:47,849
along with
all his research.

248
00:21:47,936 --> 00:21:50,634
We received a cable
from his colleague,
Dr. Schneider,

249
00:21:50,721 --> 00:21:54,595
who has no idea
of his whereabouts
or what's become of him.

250
00:21:54,682 --> 00:21:58,294
I want you to
pick up the trail
where he left off.

251
00:21:58,381 --> 00:22:00,296
Find the man,
and you will
find the Grail.

252
00:22:02,516 --> 00:22:04,605
You've got the wrong Jones,
Mr. Donovan.

253
00:22:06,998 --> 00:22:08,826
Why don't you
try my father?

254
00:22:08,913 --> 00:22:10,959
We already have.

255
00:22:11,046 --> 00:22:13,309
Your father is the man
who has disappeared.

256
00:22:26,278 --> 00:22:27,976
BRODY: Your father and I
have been friends since
time began.

257
00:22:28,063 --> 00:22:29,238
I've watched you
grow up, Indy.

258
00:22:29,325 --> 00:22:31,066
I've watched
the two of you
grow apart.

259
00:22:31,153 --> 00:22:32,676
I've never seen you
this concerned
about him before.

260
00:22:32,763 --> 00:22:34,417
Dad?

261
00:22:34,504 --> 00:22:35,984
He's an academic, a bookworm.
He's not a field man.

262
00:22:36,071 --> 00:22:36,985
Dad?

263
00:22:37,899 --> 00:22:38,639
Dad?

264
00:22:39,422 --> 00:22:40,380
Dear God.

265
00:22:44,253 --> 00:22:46,473
BRODY:
What has the old fool
got himself into now?

266
00:22:48,562 --> 00:22:50,564
I don't know,
but whatever it is,
he's in over his head.

267
00:22:51,608 --> 00:22:53,567
Dad?

268
00:22:53,654 --> 00:22:55,656
It's today's mail,
and it's been opened.

269
00:22:58,485 --> 00:22:59,355
Mail.

270
00:23:00,356 --> 00:23:01,662
That's it, Marcus.

271
00:23:04,404 --> 00:23:05,579
Venice, Italy.

272
00:23:09,278 --> 00:23:10,322
BRODY: What is it?

273
00:23:14,239 --> 00:23:15,458
It's Dad's
Grail diary.

274
00:23:18,374 --> 00:23:21,551
Every clue he followed.
Every discovery he made.

275
00:23:21,638 --> 00:23:23,074
A complete record
of his search for
the Holy Grail.

276
00:23:25,250 --> 00:23:27,905
This is his whole life.

277
00:23:27,992 --> 00:23:30,125
Why would he have
sent this to me?

278
00:23:30,212 --> 00:23:31,996
I don't know,

279
00:23:32,083 --> 00:23:34,042
but someone must
want it pretty badly.

280
00:23:44,661 --> 00:23:46,097
Do you believe, Marcus?

281
00:23:59,110 --> 00:24:00,982
Do you believe the Grail
actually exists?

282
00:24:03,724 --> 00:24:08,032
The search for the
Cup of Christ is the search
for the divine in all of us.

283
00:24:08,119 --> 00:24:09,860
But if you want facts, Indy,
I've none to give you.

284
00:24:12,167 --> 00:24:14,604
At my age,
I'm prepared to take
a few things on faith.

285
00:24:20,958 --> 00:24:22,482
Call Donovan, Marcus.

286
00:24:22,569 --> 00:24:24,745
Tell him I'll take that
ticket to Venice now.

287
00:24:26,877 --> 00:24:28,444
I'll tell him
we'll take two.

288
00:24:34,537 --> 00:24:37,105
All right, tell me
what's going to happen
when we get to Venice.

289
00:24:37,192 --> 00:24:39,063
Don't worry,
Dr. Schneider will
be there to meet you.

290
00:24:39,150 --> 00:24:40,064
Schneider? I maintain an apartment
in Venice.

291
00:24:40,151 --> 00:24:41,457
It's at your disposal.

292
00:24:41,544 --> 00:24:42,806
Oh, well, that's good.
Thank you.

293
00:24:45,417 --> 00:24:48,899
Dr. Jones, good luck.

294
00:24:48,986 --> 00:24:51,075
Now be very careful.
Don't trust anybody.

295
00:25:25,632 --> 00:25:27,198
Signora? Signorina?

296
00:25:35,206 --> 00:25:39,167
Ah, Venice...Yes.

297
00:25:39,254 --> 00:25:43,084
How will we recognize
this Dr. Schneider
when we see him?

298
00:25:43,171 --> 00:25:45,303
I don't know.
Maybe he'll know us.

299
00:25:45,390 --> 00:25:46,348
WOMAN: Dr. Jones?

300
00:25:48,219 --> 00:25:49,177
INDY: Yes?

301
00:25:50,178 --> 00:25:51,483
I knew it was you.

302
00:25:52,746 --> 00:25:54,312
You have your
father's eyes.

303
00:25:57,272 --> 00:26:01,058
And my mother's ears,
but the rest belongs
to you.

304
00:26:01,145 --> 00:26:02,843
Looks like the best
parts have already
been spoken for.

305
00:26:04,105 --> 00:26:05,106
Marcus Brody?

306
00:26:05,193 --> 00:26:05,933
That's right.

307
00:26:06,847 --> 00:26:08,022
Dr. Elsa Schneider.

308
00:26:08,109 --> 00:26:08,936
How do you do?

309
00:26:10,764 --> 00:26:13,767
ELSA: The last time
I saw your father,
we were in the library.

310
00:26:13,854 --> 00:26:16,334
He was very close
to tracking down
the knight's tomb.

311
00:26:16,421 --> 00:26:18,162
[LAUGHS] I've never
seen him so excited.

312
00:26:18,249 --> 00:26:20,512
He was as giddy
as a schoolboy.

313
00:26:20,600 --> 00:26:22,645
INDY: Who?
Attila the Professor?

314
00:26:22,732 --> 00:26:25,126
He was never giddy,
even when he was
a schoolboy.

315
00:26:26,431 --> 00:26:28,869
Fraulein,
will you permit me?

316
00:26:28,956 --> 00:26:30,479
I usually don't. I usually don't, either.

317
00:26:30,566 --> 00:26:32,002
In that case,
I permit you.

318
00:26:32,089 --> 00:26:33,395
It would make me
very happy.

319
00:26:33,482 --> 00:26:35,397
But I'm already sad.
By tomorrow,
it will have faded.

320
00:26:35,484 --> 00:26:36,790
Tomorrow
I'll steal you another.

321
00:26:36,877 --> 00:26:38,966
I hate to interrupt you,
but the reason we're here...

322
00:26:39,053 --> 00:26:41,098
Yes. I have something
to show you.

323
00:26:41,185 --> 00:26:43,405
I left your father
working in the library.

324
00:26:43,492 --> 00:26:46,669
He sent me
to the map section to fetch
an ancient plan of the city.

325
00:26:46,756 --> 00:26:49,454
When I got back to
his table, he'd gone,
with all his papers,

326
00:26:49,541 --> 00:26:51,239
except for that scrap,
which I found
near his chair.

327
00:26:51,326 --> 00:26:53,197
Roman numerals.

328
00:26:53,284 --> 00:26:55,460
ELSA:
Here is the library.

329
00:26:55,547 --> 00:26:58,159
INDY: That doesn't look
much like a library.

330
00:26:58,246 --> 00:27:00,291
BRODY: Looks like
a converted church.

331
00:27:00,378 --> 00:27:02,250
In this case,
it's the literal truth.

332
00:27:02,337 --> 00:27:04,600
We are on holy ground.

333
00:27:04,687 --> 00:27:08,256
These columns over here
were brought back
as spoils of war

334
00:27:08,343 --> 00:27:10,737
after the sacking
of Byzantium
during the Crusades.

335
00:27:12,521 --> 00:27:14,392
Now, please excuse me.

336
00:27:14,479 --> 00:27:16,264
The library's closing
in a few moments.

337
00:27:16,351 --> 00:27:18,527
I'll arrange for us
to stay a little longer.

338
00:27:21,748 --> 00:27:24,968
Marcus, I've seen
this window before.

339
00:27:25,055 --> 00:27:26,448
Where?

340
00:27:26,535 --> 00:27:28,058
Right here,
in Dad's diary.

341
00:27:36,937 --> 00:27:38,416
INDY: You see?

342
00:27:38,503 --> 00:27:40,680
Look, Indy.
The Roman numerals.

343
00:27:42,116 --> 00:27:44,422
Dad was onto
something here.

344
00:27:44,509 --> 00:27:46,076
Well, now we know
the source of the numbers,

345
00:27:46,163 --> 00:27:48,078
but we still don't know
what they mean.

346
00:27:48,165 --> 00:27:50,037
Dad sent me this diary
for a reason.

347
00:27:50,124 --> 00:27:52,169
Until we find out why,
I suggest we keep it
to ourselves.

348
00:27:52,256 --> 00:27:53,823
Find something?

349
00:27:53,910 --> 00:27:56,347
Yes. Three,
seven and ten.

350
00:27:56,434 --> 00:27:58,306
That window seems
to be the source of
the Roman numerals.

351
00:27:58,393 --> 00:28:00,395
My God,
I must be blind.

352
00:28:00,482 --> 00:28:03,354
Dad wasn't looking
for a book about
the knight's tomb,

353
00:28:03,441 --> 00:28:05,095
he was looking
for the tomb itself.

354
00:28:05,182 --> 00:28:06,836
Don't you get it?

355
00:28:06,923 --> 00:28:08,925
The tomb is somewhere
in the library.

356
00:28:09,012 --> 00:28:10,405
You said yourself
it used to be a church.

357
00:28:10,492 --> 00:28:11,406
Look.

358
00:28:11,928 --> 00:28:12,842
Three.

359
00:28:15,366 --> 00:28:16,367
Three.

360
00:28:16,933 --> 00:28:18,239
Seven.

361
00:28:18,326 --> 00:28:19,544
Seven. Ten.

362
00:28:21,590 --> 00:28:22,896
And ten.

363
00:28:22,983 --> 00:28:24,332
Now where's the ten?

364
00:28:26,551 --> 00:28:27,988
Look around
for the ten.

365
00:28:37,562 --> 00:28:39,390
INDY: Three and seven.

366
00:28:39,477 --> 00:28:40,914
Seven and seven

367
00:28:42,132 --> 00:28:43,003
and ten.

368
00:29:03,937 --> 00:29:04,720
Ten.

369
00:29:07,288 --> 00:29:08,550
"X" marks the spot.

370
00:29:34,532 --> 00:29:35,751
[LOUD BANGING]

371
00:30:06,303 --> 00:30:07,870
Bingo.

372
00:30:07,957 --> 00:30:09,567
You don't disappoint,
Dr. Jones.

373
00:30:09,654 --> 00:30:11,091
You're a great deal
like your father.

374
00:30:11,178 --> 00:30:12,483
Except he's lost,
and I'm not.

375
00:30:12,570 --> 00:30:13,789
Lower me down.

376
00:30:24,495 --> 00:30:26,193
Look after this
for me, will you?

377
00:30:40,337 --> 00:30:41,295
Come on.

378
00:30:49,825 --> 00:30:52,088
Pagan symbols.
Fourth or fifth century.

379
00:30:52,175 --> 00:30:53,655
Right.

380
00:30:53,742 --> 00:30:56,919
600 years
before the Crusades.

381
00:30:57,006 --> 00:30:58,660
The Christians would have
dug their own passages

382
00:30:58,747 --> 00:31:00,662
and burial chambers
centuries later.

383
00:31:00,749 --> 00:31:02,229
That's right.

384
00:31:02,316 --> 00:31:04,622
If there's a knight of
the First Crusade down here,

385
00:31:04,709 --> 00:31:05,667
that's where
we'll find him.

386
00:31:22,989 --> 00:31:23,728
[BRODY GRUNTS]

387
00:31:38,178 --> 00:31:40,006
What's this one?

388
00:31:40,093 --> 00:31:41,659
The Ark of the Covenant.

389
00:31:41,746 --> 00:31:43,270
Are you sure?Pretty sure.

390
00:32:01,288 --> 00:32:02,332
Watch out.

391
00:32:18,131 --> 00:32:19,741
It's petroleum.

392
00:32:19,828 --> 00:32:21,873
I should sink a well
down here and retire.

393
00:32:39,543 --> 00:32:40,196
Give me the lighter.

394
00:32:50,990 --> 00:32:52,034
Oh, rats.

395
00:32:54,471 --> 00:32:56,125
[SQUEAKING]

396
00:33:06,483 --> 00:33:07,267
[SCREAMS]

397
00:33:08,746 --> 00:33:09,791
Come on.

398
00:33:58,927 --> 00:34:00,059
Come here.

399
00:34:08,806 --> 00:34:12,506
Look, it...
it must be one of these.

400
00:34:12,593 --> 00:34:15,335
ELSA: Look at the artistry
of these carvings,
and the scrollwork.

401
00:34:32,003 --> 00:34:33,266
It's this one.

402
00:34:49,369 --> 00:34:51,414
This is it.
We found it.

403
00:34:55,244 --> 00:34:56,158
Look.

404
00:34:57,594 --> 00:34:59,379
The engraving
on the shield,

405
00:34:59,466 --> 00:35:01,163
it's the same as
on the Grail tablet.

406
00:35:03,861 --> 00:35:06,168
The shield is
the second marker.

407
00:35:06,255 --> 00:35:08,127
What's that?

408
00:35:08,214 --> 00:35:10,433
It's a rubbing Dad made
of the Grail tablet.

409
00:35:13,132 --> 00:35:15,438
Just like your father,
giddy as a schoolboy.

410
00:35:18,180 --> 00:35:19,138
Wouldn't it be wonderful
if he were here now
to see this?

411
00:35:21,923 --> 00:35:23,751
He never would have
made it past the rats.

412
00:35:23,838 --> 00:35:25,274
He hates rats.

413
00:35:25,361 --> 00:35:26,536
He's scared
to death of them.

414
00:35:34,979 --> 00:35:37,156
[WHOOSHING]

415
00:35:37,243 --> 00:35:40,289
[SQUEAKING]

416
00:35:48,776 --> 00:35:50,473
Get back!
Back against the wall.

417
00:35:55,870 --> 00:35:57,306
Quick! Under it!
Air pocket!

418
00:36:04,183 --> 00:36:06,010
Don't wander off. What?

419
00:36:06,097 --> 00:36:06,968
What?

420
00:36:18,719 --> 00:36:20,329
[SCREAMING]

421
00:36:26,640 --> 00:36:28,424
I think
I've found a way out.

422
00:36:28,511 --> 00:36:29,469
Deep breath.

423
00:36:40,306 --> 00:36:41,132
[EXCLAIMING]

424
00:36:46,921 --> 00:36:48,749
Ah, Venice.

425
00:37:55,511 --> 00:37:57,818
Are you crazy?
Don't go between them!

426
00:37:57,905 --> 00:37:59,385
Go between them?
Are you crazy?

427
00:38:11,048 --> 00:38:13,094
I said go around!

428
00:38:13,181 --> 00:38:15,096
You said
go between them!

429
00:38:15,183 --> 00:38:17,272
I said,
don't go between them!

430
00:39:19,682 --> 00:39:20,596
No!

431
00:39:26,646 --> 00:39:28,343
Why are you
trying to kill us?

432
00:39:28,430 --> 00:39:31,215
Because you're looking
for the Holy Grail.

433
00:39:31,302 --> 00:39:33,566
My father was looking
for the Holy Grail.
Did you kill him too?

434
00:39:33,653 --> 00:39:34,784
KAZIM: No.

435
00:39:34,871 --> 00:39:37,178
Where is he?

436
00:39:37,265 --> 00:39:38,832
Talk or you're dead.

437
00:39:38,919 --> 00:39:40,137
Damn it, tell me!

438
00:39:41,095 --> 00:39:42,966
Tell me!

439
00:39:43,053 --> 00:39:44,925
If you don't
let go, Dr. Jones,
we'll both die.

440
00:39:45,012 --> 00:39:47,057
Then we'll die.

441
00:39:47,144 --> 00:39:49,103
My soul is prepared.
How's yours?

442
00:39:51,627 --> 00:39:52,976
This is your
last chance.

443
00:39:53,063 --> 00:39:54,717
No, Dr. Jones,
it's yours.

444
00:40:11,952 --> 00:40:14,650
INDY: All right,
where's my father?

445
00:40:14,737 --> 00:40:16,696
KAZIM: If you let me go,
I will tell you where he is.

446
00:40:17,740 --> 00:40:19,481
Who are you?

447
00:40:19,568 --> 00:40:21,309
My name is Kazim.

448
00:40:21,396 --> 00:40:23,964
And why were you
trying to kill me?

449
00:40:24,051 --> 00:40:27,707
The secret of the Grail
has been safe
for a thousand years,

450
00:40:27,794 --> 00:40:29,796
and for all that time,

451
00:40:29,883 --> 00:40:32,059
the Brotherhood of
the Cruciform Sword

452
00:40:32,146 --> 00:40:34,453
have been prepared
to do anything
to keep it safe.

453
00:40:37,586 --> 00:40:39,066
Let me off
at this jetty.

454
00:40:43,157 --> 00:40:48,075
Ask yourself,
why do you seek
the Cup of Christ?

455
00:40:48,162 --> 00:40:49,990
Is it for His glory,
or for yours?

456
00:40:52,427 --> 00:40:54,734
I didn't come
for the Cup of Christ.
I came to find my father.

457
00:40:57,301 --> 00:40:59,869
In that case,
God be with you
in your quest.

458
00:40:59,956 --> 00:41:01,697
Your father is being held
in the Castle of Brunwald,

459
00:41:01,784 --> 00:41:03,569
on the
Austrian-German border.

460
00:41:12,839 --> 00:41:14,318
INDY: How's the head?

461
00:41:14,405 --> 00:41:16,190
BRODY: It's better,
now I've seen this.

462
00:41:18,235 --> 00:41:20,368
It's the name of a city.
"Alexandretta"?

463
00:41:24,851 --> 00:41:26,156
The knights of
the First Crusade

464
00:41:26,243 --> 00:41:29,508
laid siege to the city
of Alexandretta for
over a year.

465
00:41:29,595 --> 00:41:31,074
The entire city
was destroyed.

466
00:41:35,078 --> 00:41:37,428
The present city
of Iskenderun is
built on its ruins.

467
00:41:39,692 --> 00:41:42,390
Marcus, you remember
what the Grail tablet said,

468
00:41:42,477 --> 00:41:44,261
"Across the desert
and through the mountain

469
00:41:44,348 --> 00:41:46,176
"to the Canyon of
the Crescent Moon."

470
00:41:49,353 --> 00:41:51,312
But where exactly?

471
00:41:51,399 --> 00:41:52,792
Your father
would know.

472
00:41:55,621 --> 00:41:59,407
Your father did know.
Look. He made a map.

473
00:41:59,494 --> 00:42:00,974
He must have pieced it
together from clues
scattered through

474
00:42:01,061 --> 00:42:03,150
the whole history
of the Grail quest.

475
00:42:03,237 --> 00:42:04,717
A map with no names.

476
00:42:04,804 --> 00:42:08,634
Now, he knew there was
a city with an oasis
due east, here.

477
00:42:08,721 --> 00:42:11,375
He knew the course
turned south through
the desert to a river,

478
00:42:11,462 --> 00:42:12,855
and the river led
into the mountains, here.

479
00:42:12,942 --> 00:42:14,640
Straight to the canyon.

480
00:42:14,727 --> 00:42:16,859
He knew everything
except where to begin,

481
00:42:16,946 --> 00:42:18,339
the name of the city.

482
00:42:20,428 --> 00:42:22,691
Alexandretta.

483
00:42:22,778 --> 00:42:25,520
Now we know. Yes. Now we know.

484
00:42:25,607 --> 00:42:27,174
Marcus, get hold of Sallah.
Tell him to meet you
in Iskenderun.

485
00:42:27,261 --> 00:42:28,915
What about you?

486
00:42:29,002 --> 00:42:29,568
I'm going after Dad.

487
00:42:51,807 --> 00:42:53,330
Elsa?

488
00:42:53,417 --> 00:42:55,028
[MUSIC PLAYING
ON PHONOGRAPH]

489
00:42:59,119 --> 00:43:00,599
Elsa? Elsa?

490
00:43:00,686 --> 00:43:01,556
[GASPS]

491
00:43:12,611 --> 00:43:14,134
My room... Mine, too.

492
00:43:15,701 --> 00:43:16,745
What were they
looking for?

493
00:43:16,832 --> 00:43:18,617
This.

494
00:43:18,704 --> 00:43:19,705
The Grail diary? Uh-huh.

495
00:43:21,620 --> 00:43:22,708
You had it?

496
00:43:24,231 --> 00:43:25,711
You didn't trust me.

497
00:43:25,798 --> 00:43:27,930
I didn't know you.

498
00:43:28,017 --> 00:43:30,063
At least
I let you tag along.

499
00:43:30,150 --> 00:43:31,934
Oh, yes.

500
00:43:32,021 --> 00:43:33,936
Give them a flower,
and they'll follow you
anywhere.

501
00:43:36,156 --> 00:43:37,766
Knock it off.
You're not mad.

502
00:43:38,549 --> 00:43:39,986
No?

503
00:43:40,073 --> 00:43:43,032
No. You like the way
I do things.

504
00:43:43,119 --> 00:43:44,817
It's lucky
I don't do things
the same way.

505
00:43:44,904 --> 00:43:47,950
You'd still be standing
at the Venice pier!

506
00:43:48,037 --> 00:43:51,214
Look, what do you think
is going on here?

507
00:43:51,301 --> 00:43:53,695
Since I met you,
I've nearly been
incinerated,

508
00:43:53,782 --> 00:43:56,263
drowned, shot at,
and chopped into
fish bait.

509
00:43:56,350 --> 00:43:58,004
We're caught in the middle
of something sinister here.

510
00:43:58,091 --> 00:44:00,963
My guess is Dad
found out more than
he was looking for.

511
00:44:01,050 --> 00:44:04,053
And until I'm sure,
I'm going to
continue to do things

512
00:44:04,140 --> 00:44:06,055
the way I think
they should be done.

513
00:44:09,537 --> 00:44:11,060
How dare you kiss me!

514
00:44:17,980 --> 00:44:20,504
Leave me alone.
I don't like fast women.

515
00:44:22,289 --> 00:44:24,683
And I hate arrogant men.

516
00:44:29,078 --> 00:44:31,994
[GONDOLIER SINGING]

517
00:44:35,389 --> 00:44:36,695
Ah, Venice.

518
00:44:59,326 --> 00:44:59,979
[THUNDERING]

519
00:45:09,728 --> 00:45:11,425
What do you know
about this place?

520
00:45:11,512 --> 00:45:13,819
I know the Brunwalds
are famous art collectors.

521
00:45:17,126 --> 00:45:18,867
What are you
going to do?

522
00:45:18,954 --> 00:45:20,521
Don't know.
I'll think of something.

523
00:45:25,395 --> 00:45:26,527
[KNOCKING]

524
00:45:28,355 --> 00:45:29,922
Yes?

525
00:45:30,009 --> 00:45:32,054
[IN SCOTTISH ACCENT]
And not before time!

526
00:45:32,141 --> 00:45:33,926
Did you intend to leave us
standing on the doorstep
all day?

527
00:45:34,013 --> 00:45:35,318
We're drenched!

528
00:45:35,405 --> 00:45:36,972
[FAKES A SNEEZE]

529
00:45:37,059 --> 00:45:39,496
Now look, I've gone
and caught a sniffle.

530
00:45:39,583 --> 00:45:41,324
Are you expected?

531
00:45:41,411 --> 00:45:43,979
Do not take that tone
with me, my good man.

532
00:45:44,066 --> 00:45:46,068
Now buttle off and
tell Baron Brunwald

533
00:45:46,155 --> 00:45:49,463
that Lord Clarence MacDonald
and his lovely assistant

534
00:45:49,550 --> 00:45:51,552
are here to view
the tapestries.

535
00:45:51,639 --> 00:45:53,032
Tapestries?

536
00:45:53,119 --> 00:45:54,947
Dear me,
the man is dense.

537
00:45:55,034 --> 00:45:57,471
This is a castle, isn't it?
There are tapestries?

538
00:45:58,472 --> 00:46:00,648
This is a castle,

539
00:46:00,735 --> 00:46:03,782
and we have
many tapestries.

540
00:46:03,869 --> 00:46:06,785
But if you are
a Scottish lord,
then I am Mickey Mouse!

541
00:46:08,612 --> 00:46:09,700
How dare he?

542
00:46:23,627 --> 00:46:25,586
[INDISTINCT CHATTERING]

543
00:46:25,673 --> 00:46:26,935
[TELEPHONE RINGING]

544
00:46:37,206 --> 00:46:39,295
Nazis. I hate these guys.

545
00:46:43,169 --> 00:46:44,257
[THUNDERING]

546
00:46:53,396 --> 00:46:54,745
This one.
I think he's in here.

547
00:46:54,833 --> 00:46:56,530
How do you know?

548
00:46:56,617 --> 00:46:57,966
Because it's wired.

549
00:47:26,560 --> 00:47:28,257
Indy? Indy?

550
00:47:29,345 --> 00:47:31,260
Don't worry.
This is kid's play.

551
00:47:31,347 --> 00:47:32,653
I'll be right back.

552
00:47:57,504 --> 00:47:58,505
Junior?

553
00:48:00,072 --> 00:48:01,508
Yes, sir.

554
00:48:01,595 --> 00:48:02,901
It is you, Junior!

555
00:48:03,989 --> 00:48:05,991
Don't call me that,
please.

556
00:48:06,078 --> 00:48:07,644
Well, what are you
doing here?

557
00:48:07,731 --> 00:48:09,037
I came to get you.
What do you thi...

558
00:48:09,124 --> 00:48:10,386
[VOICES APPROACHING]

559
00:48:19,787 --> 00:48:21,484
Late 14th century,
Ming dynasty.

560
00:48:23,182 --> 00:48:24,879
It breaks the heart.

561
00:48:24,966 --> 00:48:27,055
And the head.
You hit me, Dad.

562
00:48:27,142 --> 00:48:28,665
I'll never
forgive myself.

563
00:48:29,623 --> 00:48:31,059
Don't worry, I'm fine.

564
00:48:33,366 --> 00:48:34,323
Thank God.

565
00:48:37,674 --> 00:48:38,806
It's fake.

566
00:48:41,461 --> 00:48:43,202
See, you can tell
by the cross section.

567
00:48:44,899 --> 00:48:47,380
No!

568
00:48:47,467 --> 00:48:49,730
Dad, get your stuff.
We've got to get
out of here.

569
00:48:49,817 --> 00:48:51,384
HENRY: Well, I'm sorry
about your head, though,

570
00:48:51,471 --> 00:48:53,647
but I thought
you were one of them.

571
00:48:53,734 --> 00:48:55,692
Dad, they come in
through the doors.

572
00:48:56,606 --> 00:48:57,607
Good point.

573
00:48:58,957 --> 00:49:00,741
But better safe
than sorry.

574
00:49:00,828 --> 00:49:03,004
So I was wrong this time.

575
00:49:03,091 --> 00:49:06,094
But, by God, I wasn't wrong
when I mailed you my diary.

576
00:49:06,181 --> 00:49:06,877
You obviously got it.

577
00:49:10,229 --> 00:49:11,665
I got it,
and I used it.

578
00:49:13,406 --> 00:49:15,321
We found the entrance
to the catacombs.

579
00:49:15,408 --> 00:49:16,409
Through the library?

580
00:49:17,410 --> 00:49:18,237
Right.

581
00:49:21,980 --> 00:49:23,851
I knew it.

582
00:49:23,938 --> 00:49:25,070
And the tomb
of Sir Richard?

583
00:49:27,028 --> 00:49:27,986
Found it.

584
00:49:29,857 --> 00:49:31,337
He was actually there?

585
00:49:34,340 --> 00:49:36,037
You saw him?

586
00:49:36,124 --> 00:49:37,734
Well, what was
left of him.

587
00:49:38,474 --> 00:49:39,780
And his shield,

588
00:49:41,477 --> 00:49:43,566
the inscription on
Sir Richard's shield?

589
00:49:45,003 --> 00:49:46,787
"Alexandretta."

590
00:49:46,874 --> 00:49:48,919
[EXCLAIMING]
Alexandretta! Of course!

591
00:49:50,834 --> 00:49:53,098
On the pilgrim trail
from the Eastern Empire.

592
00:49:56,492 --> 00:49:57,841
Junior, you did it.

593
00:49:58,842 --> 00:50:00,757
No, Dad. You did.

594
00:50:00,844 --> 00:50:01,845
Forty years.

595
00:50:03,717 --> 00:50:05,893
If only I could
have been with you.

596
00:50:05,980 --> 00:50:07,329
There were rats, Dad.

597
00:50:08,374 --> 00:50:09,462
Rats?

598
00:50:09,549 --> 00:50:11,203
Yeah, big ones.

599
00:50:11,290 --> 00:50:12,943
What do the Nazis
want with you, Dad?

600
00:50:13,031 --> 00:50:16,208
They wanted my diary.Yeah?

601
00:50:16,295 --> 00:50:19,559
I knew I had to get that
book as far away from me
as I possibly could.

602
00:50:22,736 --> 00:50:23,519
Yeah.

603
00:50:28,785 --> 00:50:30,048
Dr. Jones.

604
00:50:30,135 --> 00:50:30,961
BOTH: Yes?

605
00:50:34,878 --> 00:50:35,966
I will take
the book now.

606
00:50:38,665 --> 00:50:40,145
BOTH: What book?

607
00:50:40,232 --> 00:50:42,538
You have the diary
in your pocket.

608
00:50:42,625 --> 00:50:45,672
[CHUCKLING] You dolt!

609
00:50:45,759 --> 00:50:48,414
Do you think my son
would be that stupid

610
00:50:48,501 --> 00:50:51,025
that he would bring
my diary all the way
back here?

611
00:50:52,896 --> 00:50:54,028
You didn't, did you?

612
00:50:55,986 --> 00:50:57,379
You didn't bring it,
did you? Well...

613
00:50:58,076 --> 00:51:00,165
You did.

614
00:51:00,252 --> 00:51:02,210
Look, can we
discuss this later?

615
00:51:02,297 --> 00:51:03,907
I should have mailed it
to the Marx Brothers.

616
00:51:03,994 --> 00:51:05,561
Will you take it easy?

617
00:51:05,648 --> 00:51:07,563
Take it easy?

618
00:51:07,650 --> 00:51:09,435
Why do you think
I sent it home
in the first place?

619
00:51:09,522 --> 00:51:11,132
So it wouldn't fall
into their hands!

620
00:51:11,219 --> 00:51:12,699
I came here
to save you!

621
00:51:12,786 --> 00:51:14,309
Oh, yeah?

622
00:51:14,396 --> 00:51:16,442
And who's gonna come
to save you, Junior?

623
00:51:16,529 --> 00:51:17,356
I told you...

624
00:51:25,320 --> 00:51:26,887
...don't call me Junior!

625
00:51:29,846 --> 00:51:31,152
Look what you did!

626
00:51:33,372 --> 00:51:35,113
I can't believe
what you did...

627
00:51:37,811 --> 00:51:40,074
Elsa? Elsa?

628
00:51:40,161 --> 00:51:42,250
That's far enough.
Put down the gun,
Dr. Jones.

629
00:51:42,337 --> 00:51:44,034
Put down the gun,
or the  Frauleindies.

630
00:51:44,122 --> 00:51:45,123
But she's one of them.

631
00:51:45,210 --> 00:51:46,646
Indy, please!

632
00:51:46,733 --> 00:51:48,474
She's a Nazi. What?

633
00:51:48,561 --> 00:51:49,649
Trust me. Indy, no!

634
00:51:49,736 --> 00:51:50,824
I will kill her!

635
00:51:50,911 --> 00:51:52,042
Yeah? Go ahead!

636
00:51:52,130 --> 00:51:53,131
No! Don't shoot!

637
00:51:54,436 --> 00:51:55,394
Don't worry.
He won't.

638
00:51:56,743 --> 00:51:58,484
Indy, please!
Do what he says!

639
00:51:58,571 --> 00:52:00,660
And don't listen to her.

640
00:52:00,747 --> 00:52:02,140
Enough! She dies! [CRYING OUT]

641
00:52:02,227 --> 00:52:03,402
Wait! Wait...

642
00:52:11,366 --> 00:52:12,411
I'm sorry.

643
00:52:13,194 --> 00:52:15,196
No, don't be.

644
00:52:29,602 --> 00:52:31,821
But you should have
listened to your father.

645
00:52:50,623 --> 00:52:52,842
She ransacked
her own room,
and I fell for it.

646
00:52:55,671 --> 00:52:57,369
How did you know
she was a Nazi?

647
00:52:59,545 --> 00:53:01,677
How did you know
she was a Nazi?

648
00:53:01,764 --> 00:53:03,201
She talks in her sleep.

649
00:53:13,907 --> 00:53:16,431
I didn't trust her.
Why did you?

650
00:53:16,518 --> 00:53:18,390
MAN: Because he didn't
take my advice.

651
00:53:25,440 --> 00:53:27,964
Donovan.

652
00:53:28,051 --> 00:53:31,490
Didn't I warn you
not to trust anybody,
Dr. Jones?

653
00:53:31,577 --> 00:53:34,493
I misjudged you, Walter.

654
00:53:34,580 --> 00:53:37,757
I knew you would
sell your mother
for an Etruscan vase,

655
00:53:37,844 --> 00:53:40,977
but I didn't know
you would sell your
country and your soul

656
00:53:41,064 --> 00:53:42,196
to the slime
of humanity.

657
00:53:44,372 --> 00:53:46,374
Dr. Schneider,

658
00:53:46,461 --> 00:53:48,289
there are pages
torn out of this.

659
00:53:57,777 --> 00:54:01,955
This book contained a map,
a map with no names,

660
00:54:02,042 --> 00:54:04,174
precise directions
from the unknown city

661
00:54:04,262 --> 00:54:05,654
to the secret Canyon
of the Crescent Moon.

662
00:54:05,741 --> 00:54:07,526
So it did.

663
00:54:07,613 --> 00:54:08,962
Where are these
missing pages,
this map?

664
00:54:09,049 --> 00:54:10,311
We must have
these pages back.

665
00:54:10,398 --> 00:54:11,530
You're wasting
your breath.

666
00:54:11,617 --> 00:54:13,358
He won't tell us,

667
00:54:13,445 --> 00:54:16,186
and he doesn't have to.

668
00:54:16,274 --> 00:54:19,973
It's perfectly obvious
where the pages are.

669
00:54:20,060 --> 00:54:21,322
He's given them
to Marcus Brody.

670
00:54:21,409 --> 00:54:23,237
HENRY: Marcus?

671
00:54:23,324 --> 00:54:25,239
You didn't drag
poor Marcus along,
did you?

672
00:54:25,326 --> 00:54:26,719
He's not up to
the challenge.

673
00:54:26,806 --> 00:54:28,024
He sticks out
like a sore thumb.

674
00:54:28,111 --> 00:54:29,199
We'll find him.

675
00:54:29,287 --> 00:54:31,332
The hell you will.

676
00:54:31,419 --> 00:54:33,116
He's got a two-day
head start on you,

677
00:54:33,203 --> 00:54:35,162
which is more
than he needs.

678
00:54:35,249 --> 00:54:36,685
Brody's got friends
in every town and village

679
00:54:36,772 --> 00:54:38,470
from here to the Sudan.

680
00:54:38,557 --> 00:54:40,646
He speaks a dozen languages,
knows every local custom.

681
00:54:40,733 --> 00:54:42,822
He'll blend in, disappear.

682
00:54:42,909 --> 00:54:44,650
You'll never
see him again.

683
00:54:44,737 --> 00:54:46,739
With any luck,
he's got the Grail already.

684
00:54:48,480 --> 00:54:50,395
Does anyone here
speak English?

685
00:54:50,482 --> 00:54:51,570
Or even ancient Greek?

686
00:54:51,657 --> 00:54:52,875
Water? No, thank you, sir.

687
00:54:52,962 --> 00:54:53,789
No. Fish make love in it.

688
00:54:55,748 --> 00:54:57,576
Goodness me.
Thank you so much.
No, I don't like that.

689
00:54:57,663 --> 00:54:59,142
No, I really
don't want...

690
00:54:59,229 --> 00:55:01,493
No, no,
thank you very much.

691
00:55:01,580 --> 00:55:03,843
No, thank you, madam.
I'm a vegetarian.

692
00:55:03,930 --> 00:55:06,280
Does anyone understand
a word I'm saying here?

693
00:55:06,367 --> 00:55:07,890
Mr. Brody!

694
00:55:07,977 --> 00:55:09,109
Sallah. What a relief.

695
00:55:09,196 --> 00:55:11,894
Marcus Brody, sir.

696
00:55:11,981 --> 00:55:13,331
But where is Indy? He's in Austria.

697
00:55:13,418 --> 00:55:14,941
A slight detour.

698
00:55:15,028 --> 00:55:16,508
You are on your own?

699
00:55:16,595 --> 00:55:19,641
Yes, but don't panic.
Everything's under control.

700
00:55:19,728 --> 00:55:21,382
Have you, have you
arranged our supplies?

701
00:55:21,469 --> 00:55:23,253
Yes, of course.
But where are we going?

702
00:55:23,341 --> 00:55:24,516
This map will show you.

703
00:55:24,603 --> 00:55:26,256
It was drawn by...

704
00:55:26,996 --> 00:55:28,171
Mr. Brody.

705
00:55:29,172 --> 00:55:30,870
Welcome to Iskenderun.

706
00:55:30,957 --> 00:55:32,611
The director
of the Museum
of Antiquities

707
00:55:32,698 --> 00:55:33,916
has sent a car for you.

708
00:55:34,003 --> 00:55:36,266
Well, your servant, sir.

709
00:55:36,354 --> 00:55:37,093
And I am his.

710
00:55:38,312 --> 00:55:40,270
Follow me, please.

711
00:55:40,358 --> 00:55:41,837
My reputation
precedes me.

712
00:55:41,924 --> 00:55:43,752
There is no museum
in Iskenderun.

713
00:55:43,839 --> 00:55:45,232
Papers, please.

714
00:55:45,319 --> 00:55:47,800
Papers? Of course.

715
00:55:47,887 --> 00:55:49,279
Run. Yes.

716
00:55:49,367 --> 00:55:51,543
Papers. Got it here.

717
00:55:51,630 --> 00:55:52,761
Just finished
reading it myself.

718
00:55:52,848 --> 00:55:55,503
Run. Yes.

719
00:55:55,590 --> 00:55:56,983
Egyptian Mail,
morning edition. Run.

720
00:55:57,070 --> 00:55:58,332
Did you say...

721
00:55:58,419 --> 00:55:59,507
Run!

722
00:56:15,088 --> 00:56:17,133
Okay, okay,
quick, quick, quick!

723
00:56:17,220 --> 00:56:19,353
Find the back door!
Find the back door!

724
00:56:32,932 --> 00:56:34,499
Intolerable.

725
00:56:37,632 --> 00:56:39,460
Dr. Schneider.
Message from Berlin.

726
00:56:39,547 --> 00:56:41,331
You must return
immediately.

727
00:56:41,419 --> 00:56:42,637
A rally at the Institute
of Aryan Culture.

728
00:56:42,724 --> 00:56:44,596
So?

729
00:56:44,683 --> 00:56:48,774
Your presence on the
platform is requested
at the highest level.

730
00:56:48,861 --> 00:56:51,733
Thank you,  HerrOberst.
I will meet you
at Iskenderun.

731
00:56:51,820 --> 00:56:53,779
Take this diary
to the Reichmuseum
in Berlin.

732
00:56:53,866 --> 00:56:56,346
It will show
them our progress,
ahead of schedule.

733
00:56:56,434 --> 00:56:58,348
Without the map,
I'm afraid it's no
better than a souvenir.

734
00:56:58,436 --> 00:56:59,611
Let me kill them now.

735
00:56:59,698 --> 00:57:01,264
No.

736
00:57:01,351 --> 00:57:02,831
If we fail to recover
the pages from Brody,

737
00:57:02,918 --> 00:57:04,703
we'll need them alive.

738
00:57:04,790 --> 00:57:06,661
Always do what
the doctor orders.

739
00:57:15,148 --> 00:57:16,062
Don't look at me
like that.

740
00:57:17,367 --> 00:57:19,369
We both wanted the Grail.

741
00:57:19,457 --> 00:57:21,284
I would have done
anything to get it.

742
00:57:21,371 --> 00:57:23,243
You would have
done the same.

743
00:57:23,330 --> 00:57:24,766
I'm sorry
you think so.

744
00:57:36,778 --> 00:57:39,912
I can't forget
how wonderful it was.

745
00:57:39,999 --> 00:57:41,261
Thank you.

746
00:57:41,348 --> 00:57:42,741
It was rather wonderful.

747
00:57:52,185 --> 00:57:53,752
VOGEL: Dr. Schneider.

748
00:57:55,928 --> 00:57:56,842
Your car is waiting.

749
00:58:05,677 --> 00:58:07,113
That's how
Austrians say good-bye.

750
00:58:14,555 --> 00:58:17,515
And this is how
we say good-bye
in Germany, Dr. Jones.

751
00:58:18,820 --> 00:58:19,778
[GROANS]

752
00:58:26,088 --> 00:58:27,960
I liked
the Austrian way better.

753
00:58:28,047 --> 00:58:30,397
So did I.

754
00:58:30,484 --> 00:58:31,877
Let's try and get
these ropes loose.

755
00:58:31,964 --> 00:58:34,749
We've got to get to Marcus
before the Nazis do.

756
00:58:34,836 --> 00:58:36,229
You said he had
two days' start.

757
00:58:36,316 --> 00:58:38,448
That he would blend in.
Disappear.

758
00:58:38,536 --> 00:58:40,233
Are you kidding?
I made that up.

759
00:58:40,320 --> 00:58:42,627
You know Marcus.
He got lost once
in his own museum.

760
00:58:45,804 --> 00:58:47,675
Can you try and reach
my left jacket pocket?

761
00:58:49,547 --> 00:58:50,504
HENRY:
What am I looking for?

762
00:58:50,591 --> 00:58:51,679
My lucky charm.

763
00:58:54,073 --> 00:58:54,813
Feels like
a cigarette lighter.

764
00:58:56,945 --> 00:58:57,511
INDY: Try and burn
through the ropes.

765
00:58:59,644 --> 00:59:00,645
Very good.

766
00:59:06,302 --> 00:59:07,173
[HENRY EXCLAIMS]

767
00:59:26,105 --> 00:59:27,193
I ought to
tell you something.

768
00:59:27,280 --> 00:59:28,629
Don't get
sentimental now, Dad.

769
00:59:28,716 --> 00:59:30,239
Save it till we
get out of here.

770
00:59:30,326 --> 00:59:31,589
The floor's on fire.

771
00:59:31,676 --> 00:59:32,807
See? What?

772
00:59:33,982 --> 00:59:35,680
And the chair.

773
00:59:35,767 --> 00:59:38,030
Move! Move it
out of here! Go!

774
00:59:38,117 --> 00:59:39,597
It's scorching
the table! Fast!

775
00:59:39,684 --> 00:59:40,249
Move! Okay!

776
00:59:51,304 --> 00:59:52,261
[SPEAKING GERMAN]

777
00:59:55,090 --> 00:59:57,702
Well, we have
Marcus Brody,

778
00:59:57,789 --> 01:00:00,139
but more important,
we have the map.

779
01:00:00,226 --> 01:00:01,444
[SPEAKING GERMAN]

780
01:00:04,404 --> 01:00:05,840
"By the personal command
of the  Fuhrer.

781
01:00:05,927 --> 01:00:08,016
"Secrecy essential
to success.

782
01:00:08,103 --> 01:00:09,844
"Eliminate
the American conspirators."

783
01:00:11,454 --> 01:00:14,370
Germany has declared war
on the Jones boys.

784
01:00:14,457 --> 01:00:15,589
[SPEAKING GERMAN]

785
01:00:20,072 --> 01:00:21,247
Dad! What?

786
01:00:21,334 --> 01:00:22,335
Dad! What?

787
01:00:22,422 --> 01:00:23,684
Dad! What?

788
01:00:23,771 --> 01:00:24,990
Head for the fireplace!

789
01:00:35,696 --> 01:00:36,523
[COUGHING]

790
01:00:38,960 --> 01:00:40,135
INDY: I think I can
get these ropes off.

791
01:00:40,222 --> 01:00:42,268
[RUMBLING]

792
01:00:42,355 --> 01:00:43,748
Whoops!

793
01:00:43,835 --> 01:00:45,880
[SPEAKING GERMAN]

794
01:00:53,932 --> 01:00:55,890
Our situation
has not improved.

795
01:00:55,977 --> 01:00:57,718
Listen, Dad,
I'm almost free.

796
01:01:13,778 --> 01:01:15,823
[SHOUTING] Alarm!

797
01:01:15,910 --> 01:01:16,824
[ALARM RINGING]

798
01:01:24,179 --> 01:01:25,746
This is intolerable.

799
01:01:26,878 --> 01:01:27,879
I'm out, Dad.

800
01:01:27,966 --> 01:01:29,358
Well done, boy.

801
01:01:39,325 --> 01:01:41,370
[ALL SHOUTING]

802
01:01:59,867 --> 01:02:00,781
[SPEAKS GERMAN]

803
01:02:05,830 --> 01:02:07,266
Come on, Dad.

804
01:02:13,794 --> 01:02:15,709
Dead end.

805
01:02:15,796 --> 01:02:17,102
There's got to be a...

806
01:02:17,189 --> 01:02:19,974
A secret door or a...

807
01:02:20,061 --> 01:02:21,497
Passageway
or something.

808
01:02:21,584 --> 01:02:23,978
I find that if I just
sit down and think...

809
01:02:26,981 --> 01:02:29,201
[SCREAMING] Dad!

810
01:02:33,292 --> 01:02:34,772
...the solution
presents itself.

811
01:02:42,910 --> 01:02:45,217
Great. More boats.

812
01:02:49,569 --> 01:02:51,789
You say this has been
just another typical day
for you, huh?

813
01:02:52,267 --> 01:02:53,878
[GRUNTS]

814
01:02:53,965 --> 01:02:56,402
No! But better than most.

815
01:02:59,318 --> 01:03:01,450
Come on, Dad. Come on.

816
01:03:01,537 --> 01:03:03,496
What about the boat?

817
01:03:03,583 --> 01:03:04,062
We're not going
on the boat?

818
01:03:19,468 --> 01:03:20,252
Jones!

819
01:03:25,779 --> 01:03:26,780
[SPEAKS GERMAN]

820
01:04:00,640 --> 01:04:01,641
[GUNSHOTS]

821
01:04:33,281 --> 01:04:33,978
Halt! Halt!

822
01:04:46,294 --> 01:04:47,034
[GROANS]

823
01:05:37,476 --> 01:05:38,433
HENRY: Stop! What?

824
01:05:38,520 --> 01:05:41,697
Stop! Stop!

825
01:05:41,784 --> 01:05:44,918
You're going the wrong way.
We have to get to Berlin.

826
01:05:45,005 --> 01:05:46,485
Brody's this way.

827
01:05:46,572 --> 01:05:48,617
My diary's in Berlin.

828
01:05:48,704 --> 01:05:50,358
We don't need
the diary, Dad.

829
01:05:50,445 --> 01:05:52,273
Marcus has the map.

830
01:05:52,360 --> 01:05:55,363
There is more in the diary
than just the map.

831
01:05:55,450 --> 01:05:57,800
All right, Dad.
Tell me.

832
01:06:00,542 --> 01:06:04,546
Well, he who finds
the Grail must face
the final challenge.

833
01:06:04,633 --> 01:06:06,635
What final challenge?

834
01:06:06,722 --> 01:06:09,421
Three devices of
such lethal cunning.

835
01:06:09,508 --> 01:06:10,552
Booby traps? Oh, yes.

836
01:06:12,859 --> 01:06:16,210
But I found the clues that
will safely take us through,

837
01:06:16,297 --> 01:06:18,256
in the
Chronicles of St. Anselm.

838
01:06:18,343 --> 01:06:19,474
Well, what are they?

839
01:06:23,435 --> 01:06:24,436
Can't you remember?

840
01:06:26,960 --> 01:06:28,875
I wrote them down
in my diary

841
01:06:28,962 --> 01:06:31,443
so that I wouldn't
have to remember.

842
01:06:31,530 --> 01:06:32,835
Half the German army's
on our tail,

843
01:06:32,922 --> 01:06:34,489
and you want me
to go to Berlin?

844
01:06:34,576 --> 01:06:36,752
Into the lion's den? Yes.

845
01:06:36,839 --> 01:06:38,624
The only thing that
matters is the Grail.

846
01:06:38,711 --> 01:06:40,234
What about Marcus?

847
01:06:40,321 --> 01:06:41,453
Marcus would
agree with me.

848
01:06:42,932 --> 01:06:44,717
Two selfless martyrs.

849
01:06:44,804 --> 01:06:46,153
Jesus Christ.

850
01:06:46,240 --> 01:06:47,154
[GASPS]

851
01:06:49,983 --> 01:06:52,029
That's for blasphemy.

852
01:06:55,510 --> 01:06:58,426
The quest for the Grail
is not archeology.

853
01:07:00,559 --> 01:07:02,909
It's a race
against evil.

854
01:07:02,996 --> 01:07:06,043
If it is captured
by the Nazis,

855
01:07:06,130 --> 01:07:09,133
the armies of darkness
will march all over
the face of the Earth.

856
01:07:09,220 --> 01:07:09,872
Do you understand me?

857
01:07:12,049 --> 01:07:14,442
This is an obsession, Dad.

858
01:07:14,529 --> 01:07:17,184
I never understood it.
Never.

859
01:07:19,926 --> 01:07:21,754
Neither did Mom.

860
01:07:21,841 --> 01:07:23,451
Oh, yes, she did.

861
01:07:23,538 --> 01:07:25,149
Only too well.

862
01:07:27,368 --> 01:07:29,979
Unfortunately,
she kept her
illness from me

863
01:07:32,069 --> 01:07:32,982
until all I could do
was mourn her.

864
01:07:49,216 --> 01:07:51,088
[CHANTING]

865
01:07:51,175 --> 01:07:52,045
[BAND PLAYING]

866
01:08:04,884 --> 01:08:06,407
[INDISTINCT SHOUTING]

867
01:08:36,350 --> 01:08:39,048
My boy, we are pilgrims
in an unholy land.

868
01:08:46,186 --> 01:08:47,840
[CLAMORING]

869
01:09:09,209 --> 01:09:11,820
FrauleinDoctor.

870
01:09:11,907 --> 01:09:13,605
Where is it? How did you get here?

871
01:09:13,692 --> 01:09:15,259
Where is it? I want it.

872
01:09:19,350 --> 01:09:22,614
You came back
for the book? Why?

873
01:09:22,701 --> 01:09:24,485
My father didn't
want it incinerated.

874
01:09:24,572 --> 01:09:26,008
Is that what
you think of me?

875
01:09:26,095 --> 01:09:28,620
I believe in the Grail,
not the swastika.

876
01:09:28,707 --> 01:09:30,099
But you stood up
to be counted

877
01:09:30,187 --> 01:09:32,189
with the enemy of everything
that the Grail stands for.

878
01:09:32,276 --> 01:09:33,581
Who gives a damn
what you think?

879
01:09:33,668 --> 01:09:36,193
You do.

880
01:09:36,280 --> 01:09:39,283
All I have to do
is squeeze.

881
01:09:39,370 --> 01:09:41,415
All I have to do
is scream.

882
01:09:51,773 --> 01:09:54,036
I've got it.
Let's get the hell
out of here.

883
01:10:53,748 --> 01:10:55,402
[SPEAKING GERMAN]

884
01:11:07,371 --> 01:11:08,415
What did you get?I don't know.

885
01:11:08,502 --> 01:11:09,590
First available flight
out of Germany.

886
01:11:09,677 --> 01:11:11,200
Good.

887
01:11:37,575 --> 01:11:39,577
Well, we made it.

888
01:11:41,361 --> 01:11:44,277
When we're airborne,
with Germany behind us,

889
01:11:44,364 --> 01:11:46,279
then I'll share
that sentiment.

890
01:11:46,366 --> 01:11:47,672
Relax.

891
01:11:55,157 --> 01:11:57,551
[SHOUTING]

892
01:12:14,568 --> 01:12:16,091
[SPEAKS GERMAN]

893
01:12:34,022 --> 01:12:36,024
Tickets, please.

894
01:12:36,111 --> 01:12:37,374
[SPEAKS GERMAN]

895
01:12:47,079 --> 01:12:50,125
Guten Tag, HerrJones.

896
01:12:51,649 --> 01:12:53,433
[BOTH SPEAKING GERMAN]

897
01:12:54,739 --> 01:12:56,044
Tickets, please.

898
01:13:06,446 --> 01:13:07,795
[PEOPLE MURMURING]

899
01:13:07,882 --> 01:13:09,188
WOMAN: We should
get out of here.

900
01:13:11,625 --> 01:13:12,844
No ticket.

901
01:13:15,934 --> 01:13:17,370
Ticket.

902
01:13:17,457 --> 01:13:18,327
MAN 1: Ticket! MAN 2: Ticket!

903
01:13:18,415 --> 01:13:19,111
Ticket!Ticket!

904
01:13:24,812 --> 01:13:26,161
[SHOUTING IN GERMAN]

905
01:13:36,737 --> 01:13:38,957
You know,

906
01:13:39,044 --> 01:13:42,264
sharing your adventures is
an interesting experience.

907
01:13:43,831 --> 01:13:46,138
That's not all we shared.

908
01:13:46,225 --> 01:13:48,009
It's disgraceful.
You're old enough
to be her fa...

909
01:13:48,096 --> 01:13:50,229
Her grandfather.

910
01:13:50,316 --> 01:13:51,622
Well, I'm as human
as the next man.

911
01:13:51,709 --> 01:13:53,232
I was the next man.

912
01:13:54,668 --> 01:13:55,713
[CHUCKLES]

913
01:13:56,627 --> 01:13:58,237
Of course. Well...

914
01:13:58,324 --> 01:14:01,022
Ships that pass
in the night.

915
01:14:01,109 --> 01:14:03,721
Do you remember
the last time we had
a quiet drink?

916
01:14:05,853 --> 01:14:07,464
I had a milkshake.

917
01:14:08,813 --> 01:14:10,336
What did we talk about?

918
01:14:11,859 --> 01:14:13,208
We didn't talk.

919
01:14:14,645 --> 01:14:16,211
We never talked.

920
01:14:18,387 --> 01:14:20,302
Do I detect a rebuke?

921
01:14:22,914 --> 01:14:25,351
A regret.
It was just the
two of us, Dad.

922
01:14:25,438 --> 01:14:27,962
It was a lonely
way to grow up.
For you, too.

923
01:14:28,049 --> 01:14:29,834
If you'd been an ordinary,
average father,

924
01:14:29,921 --> 01:14:31,096
like the other guys' dads,
you'd have understood that.

925
01:14:32,619 --> 01:14:34,621
Actually, I was
a wonderful father.

926
01:14:34,708 --> 01:14:37,406
When?

927
01:14:37,494 --> 01:14:39,844
Did I ever tell you
to eat up? Go to bed?

928
01:14:39,931 --> 01:14:41,193
Wash your ears?
Do your homework?

929
01:14:41,280 --> 01:14:43,282
No. I respected
your privacy,

930
01:14:43,369 --> 01:14:46,633
and I taught you
self-reliance.

931
01:14:46,720 --> 01:14:49,418
What you taught me
was that I was less
important to you

932
01:14:49,506 --> 01:14:52,291
than people who'd been
dead for 500 years
in another country.

933
01:14:52,378 --> 01:14:54,554
And I learned it so well

934
01:14:54,641 --> 01:14:57,252
that we've hardly
spoken for 20 years.

935
01:14:57,339 --> 01:14:59,472
You left
just when you were
becoming interesting.

936
01:14:59,559 --> 01:15:00,821
Unbelievable.

937
01:15:00,908 --> 01:15:02,475
Dad, how can you... Very well.

938
01:15:02,562 --> 01:15:04,172
I'm here now.

939
01:15:06,305 --> 01:15:07,175
What do you want
to talk about?

940
01:15:09,003 --> 01:15:10,352
Well, I...

941
01:15:12,964 --> 01:15:14,269
[STUTTERING]

942
01:15:16,402 --> 01:15:17,534
[CHUCKLING]

943
01:15:18,883 --> 01:15:21,059
I can't think of anything.

944
01:15:21,146 --> 01:15:22,539
Then what are you
complaining about?

945
01:15:24,628 --> 01:15:26,064
Look, we have work to do.

946
01:15:28,457 --> 01:15:30,503
When we get
to Alexandretta,

947
01:15:30,590 --> 01:15:33,637
we will face
three challenges.

948
01:15:33,724 --> 01:15:36,204
The first,
"The Breath of God.

949
01:15:36,291 --> 01:15:38,729
"Only the penitent man
will pass."

950
01:15:38,816 --> 01:15:42,341
Second, "The Word of God.

951
01:15:42,428 --> 01:15:45,083
"Only in the footsteps
of God will he proceed."

952
01:15:45,170 --> 01:15:48,565
Third, "The Path of God.

953
01:15:48,652 --> 01:15:52,786
"Only in the leap
from the lion's head
will he prove his worth."

954
01:15:54,745 --> 01:15:57,051
What does that mean?

955
01:15:57,138 --> 01:15:59,401
I don't know.
We'll find out.

956
01:16:07,061 --> 01:16:09,934
We're turning around.

957
01:16:10,021 --> 01:16:11,196
They're taking us
back to Germany.

958
01:16:19,204 --> 01:16:20,509
INDY: Well, I thought
it would take them
a lot longer

959
01:16:20,597 --> 01:16:21,554
to figure out
the radio was dead.

960
01:16:21,641 --> 01:16:22,816
Come on, Dad. Move!

961
01:16:27,778 --> 01:16:29,606
Come on, Dad.
Come on!

962
01:16:43,924 --> 01:16:45,578
I didn't know you
could fly a plane.

963
01:16:45,665 --> 01:16:46,579
Fly, yes.

964
01:16:49,103 --> 01:16:50,670
Land, no.

965
01:17:09,036 --> 01:17:11,038
[GUN FIRING]

966
01:17:16,174 --> 01:17:17,218
Dad, you're going
to have to use
the machine gun.

967
01:17:17,305 --> 01:17:18,655
Get it ready.

968
01:17:20,395 --> 01:17:21,440
Eleven o'clock!

969
01:17:22,659 --> 01:17:23,747
Dad, eleven o'clock!

970
01:17:26,097 --> 01:17:28,229
What happens at
eleven o'clock?

971
01:17:28,316 --> 01:17:29,927
Twelve, eleven, ten.

972
01:17:30,014 --> 01:17:32,233
Eleven o'clock, fire!

973
01:17:58,303 --> 01:18:00,827
Dad, are we hit?

974
01:18:00,914 --> 01:18:02,307
More or less.

975
01:18:03,264 --> 01:18:05,049
Son, I'm sorry.

976
01:18:05,136 --> 01:18:06,224
They got us.

977
01:18:09,096 --> 01:18:10,489
Hang on, Dad.
We're going in!

978
01:18:23,937 --> 01:18:25,939
Nice landing.Thanks.

979
01:18:41,825 --> 01:18:42,826
Those people are
trying to kill us!

980
01:18:42,913 --> 01:18:44,871
I know, Dad!

981
01:18:44,958 --> 01:18:48,483
Well. It's a new
experience for me.

982
01:18:48,570 --> 01:18:49,615
It happens to me
all the time.

983
01:19:20,777 --> 01:19:22,039
This is intolerable!

984
01:19:23,867 --> 01:19:24,781
This could be close.

985
01:19:34,486 --> 01:19:35,704
Faster, boy! Faster!

986
01:19:52,199 --> 01:19:53,766
Well, they don't come
any closer than that!

987
01:20:10,043 --> 01:20:12,829
Dad, he's coming back!

988
01:20:39,464 --> 01:20:40,813
[CLUCKING]

989
01:20:47,602 --> 01:20:49,517
[PILOT SCREAMING]

990
01:21:00,093 --> 01:21:02,791
I suddenly remembered
my Charlemagne.

991
01:21:02,879 --> 01:21:05,577
"Let my armies be
the rocks and the trees

992
01:21:05,664 --> 01:21:07,274
"and the birds
in the sky."

993
01:21:26,119 --> 01:21:28,905
DONOVAN: These pages are
taken from Professor Jones'
diary, Your Highness,

994
01:21:28,992 --> 01:21:30,907
and they include a map

995
01:21:30,994 --> 01:21:33,735
that pinpoints
the exact location
of the Grail.

996
01:21:33,822 --> 01:21:36,303
As you can see,
the Grail is all
but in our hands.

997
01:21:36,390 --> 01:21:38,175
However, Your Highness,

998
01:21:38,262 --> 01:21:40,786
we would not think
of crossing your soil
without your permission,

999
01:21:40,873 --> 01:21:42,701
nor of removing the Grail
from your borders

1000
01:21:42,788 --> 01:21:45,312
without suitable
compensation.

1001
01:21:48,315 --> 01:21:50,100
What have you brought?

1002
01:21:51,971 --> 01:21:52,711
[SPEAKS IN GERMAN]

1003
01:21:58,456 --> 01:22:01,415
Precious valuables,
Your Highness,

1004
01:22:01,502 --> 01:22:04,592
donated by some of
the finest families
in all of Germany.

1005
01:22:10,294 --> 01:22:13,427
Rolls-Royce Phantom Two.

1006
01:22:13,514 --> 01:22:17,562
Four-point-three liter,
30 horsepower,
six-cylinder engine,

1007
01:22:17,649 --> 01:22:20,347
with Stromberg
downdraft carburetor.

1008
01:22:20,434 --> 01:22:23,481
Can go from zero to
100 kilometers an hour

1009
01:22:23,568 --> 01:22:26,440
in 12.5 seconds.

1010
01:22:26,527 --> 01:22:27,572
And I even
like the color.

1011
01:22:29,400 --> 01:22:31,054
The keys are
in the ignition,
Your Highness.

1012
01:22:33,012 --> 01:22:35,841
You shall have
camels, horses,

1013
01:22:35,928 --> 01:22:38,235
an armed escort,
provisions,

1014
01:22:38,322 --> 01:22:40,933
desert vehicles and tanks.

1015
01:22:41,020 --> 01:22:42,413
You're welcome.

1016
01:22:58,603 --> 01:23:00,213
We have no time to lose.

1017
01:23:00,300 --> 01:23:01,954
Indiana Jones
and his father
have escaped.

1018
01:23:05,218 --> 01:23:07,046
We go this way.

1019
01:23:07,133 --> 01:23:08,134
Get that camel
out of the way.

1020
01:23:11,268 --> 01:23:13,879
What happened to Marcus,
Sallah?

1021
01:23:13,966 --> 01:23:16,360
They set out
across the desert
this afternoon.

1022
01:23:16,447 --> 01:23:18,797
I believe they took
Mr. Brody with them.

1023
01:23:20,538 --> 01:23:22,279
Now they have the map.

1024
01:23:24,455 --> 01:23:26,326
And in this
sort of race,

1025
01:23:26,413 --> 01:23:28,328
there's no silver medal
for finishing second.

1026
01:23:51,612 --> 01:23:53,832
Care to wet your
whistle, Marcus?

1027
01:23:53,919 --> 01:23:55,877
I'd rather
spit in your face.

1028
01:23:55,964 --> 01:23:57,836
But as I haven't
got any spit...

1029
01:24:00,969 --> 01:24:03,798
Must be within
three or four miles.

1030
01:24:03,885 --> 01:24:05,713
Otherwise we are
off the map.

1031
01:24:08,020 --> 01:24:11,632
Well, Marcus,
we are on the brink
of the recovery of

1032
01:24:11,719 --> 01:24:15,549
the greatest artifact
in the history of mankind.

1033
01:24:15,636 --> 01:24:18,422
You're meddling with
powers you cannot
possibly comprehend.

1034
01:24:24,167 --> 01:24:25,777
[INDY EXCLAIMS]

1035
01:24:25,864 --> 01:24:27,431
INDY: I see Brody.

1036
01:24:28,127 --> 01:24:29,215
He seems okay.

1037
01:24:32,784 --> 01:24:34,960
They've got a tank.

1038
01:24:40,748 --> 01:24:43,055
Six-pound gun.

1039
01:24:43,142 --> 01:24:44,187
What do you think
you're doing there?
Get down!

1040
01:24:45,710 --> 01:24:47,712
Dad, we're
well out of range.

1041
01:24:47,799 --> 01:24:48,495
[EXPLOSION]

1042
01:24:58,201 --> 01:24:59,027
That car belonged
to my brother-in-law.

1043
01:24:59,115 --> 01:25:00,899
Come on, come on!

1044
01:25:00,986 --> 01:25:03,162
I can't see
anyone up there.

1045
01:25:03,249 --> 01:25:04,468
Maybe it wasn't
even Jones.

1046
01:25:04,555 --> 01:25:06,818
No, it is him,
all right.

1047
01:25:06,905 --> 01:25:07,993
He's here somewhere.

1048
01:25:09,429 --> 01:25:10,996
Put Brody in the tank.

1049
01:25:18,264 --> 01:25:21,702
Well, in this sun,

1050
01:25:21,789 --> 01:25:23,574
without transportation,
they're as good as dead.

1051
01:25:23,661 --> 01:25:24,836
[GUNSHOTS]

1052
01:25:33,149 --> 01:25:34,106
It's Jones, all right.

1053
01:25:39,546 --> 01:25:41,461
Now, who are
all these people? Who cares?

1054
01:25:41,548 --> 01:25:44,682
As long as they're
keeping Donovan busy.

1055
01:25:44,769 --> 01:25:46,988
Dad, you stay here while
Sallah and I organize
some transportation.

1056
01:25:57,651 --> 01:25:58,304
[SCREAMS]

1057
01:26:13,276 --> 01:26:14,668
I'm going after
those horses. I'll take the camels.

1058
01:26:14,755 --> 01:26:15,974
I don't need camels. But, Indy...

1059
01:26:16,061 --> 01:26:17,236
No camels.

1060
01:26:28,856 --> 01:26:29,640
Who is he?

1061
01:26:31,729 --> 01:26:33,339
A messenger from God.

1062
01:26:35,167 --> 01:26:37,909
For the unrighteous,

1063
01:26:37,996 --> 01:26:40,825
the Cup of Life holds
everlasting damnation.

1064
01:27:00,366 --> 01:27:02,063
Marcus![SHOUTS]

1065
01:27:04,370 --> 01:27:06,677
"Genius of
the Restoration..."

1066
01:27:06,764 --> 01:27:09,201
"Aid our own
resuscitation."

1067
01:27:09,288 --> 01:27:10,463
Henry, what are you
doing here?

1068
01:27:10,550 --> 01:27:11,377
It's a rescue, old boy.
Come on.

1069
01:27:18,558 --> 01:27:19,385
Search him.

1070
01:27:20,865 --> 01:27:22,736
What is in this book?

1071
01:27:22,823 --> 01:27:26,610
That miserable
little diary of yours.

1072
01:27:26,697 --> 01:27:29,265
We have the map.
The book is useless.

1073
01:27:29,352 --> 01:27:31,441
And yet you come
all the way back
to Berlin to get it.

1074
01:27:31,528 --> 01:27:33,312
Why?

1075
01:27:33,399 --> 01:27:36,054
What are you hiding?

1076
01:27:36,141 --> 01:27:38,535
What does the diary
tell you that
it doesn't tell us?

1077
01:27:40,972 --> 01:27:44,584
It tells me that
goose-stepping morons
like yourself

1078
01:27:44,671 --> 01:27:46,934
should try reading books
instead of burning them.

1079
01:27:47,805 --> 01:27:48,936
DONOVAN: Colonel!

1080
01:27:49,023 --> 01:27:51,025
Jones is getting away.

1081
01:27:51,112 --> 01:27:52,331
I think not,

1082
01:27:52,418 --> 01:27:53,941
HerrDonovan.

1083
01:27:54,028 --> 01:27:56,248
Not that Jones,
the other Jones!

1084
01:28:05,692 --> 01:28:08,260
Sallah, I said no camels!

1085
01:28:08,347 --> 01:28:10,654
That's five camels.
Can't you count?

1086
01:28:10,741 --> 01:28:12,960
Compensation for
my brother-in-law's car.

1087
01:28:13,047 --> 01:28:15,049
Indy, your father
and Brody...

1088
01:28:15,136 --> 01:28:16,355
Where's my father?

1089
01:28:16,442 --> 01:28:18,139
They have them.

1090
01:28:18,226 --> 01:28:19,532
In the belly of
that steel beast.

1091
01:28:37,768 --> 01:28:38,595
[SPEAKING GERMAN]

1092
01:28:43,034 --> 01:28:43,774
Fire!

1093
01:29:26,730 --> 01:29:27,992
[HORNS HONKING]

1094
01:29:29,167 --> 01:29:29,820
[SCREAMS]

1095
01:30:45,069 --> 01:30:47,288
Dad! Dad!

1096
01:30:47,375 --> 01:30:48,464
Dad!

1097
01:30:48,551 --> 01:30:49,595
Junior?

1098
01:30:49,682 --> 01:30:51,292
Junior?

1099
01:30:51,379 --> 01:30:52,206
Junior!

1100
01:30:58,517 --> 01:30:59,387
[COUGHING]

1101
01:31:42,343 --> 01:31:44,432
[SHOUTING]

1102
01:32:07,978 --> 01:32:09,414
Dad!

1103
01:32:10,328 --> 01:32:11,634
Dad!

1104
01:32:11,721 --> 01:32:13,113
Dad! Get out!

1105
01:32:32,829 --> 01:32:34,221
[SHOUTING]

1106
01:32:49,019 --> 01:32:50,586
[GRUNTING]

1107
01:32:53,284 --> 01:32:55,329
[SPEAKING GERMAN]

1108
01:33:33,933 --> 01:33:35,500
Henry, the pen...

1109
01:33:35,587 --> 01:33:37,284
What? But don't you see?

1110
01:33:37,371 --> 01:33:39,852
The pen is mightier
than the sword.

1111
01:34:02,745 --> 01:34:04,050
Look what you did.

1112
01:34:04,137 --> 01:34:05,399
It's war.

1113
01:34:11,841 --> 01:34:13,712
Didn't I tell you
it was a rescue?

1114
01:34:34,080 --> 01:34:35,168
[SHOUTING]

1115
01:35:18,124 --> 01:35:19,822
Dad! Yeah?

1116
01:35:21,258 --> 01:35:22,694
You call this archeology?

1117
01:35:22,781 --> 01:35:23,564
Get out of there, Dad.

1118
01:35:35,663 --> 01:35:36,577
How does one get off
this thing?

1119
01:35:43,933 --> 01:35:44,629
Where's Marcus?

1120
01:35:48,198 --> 01:35:48,807
[HENRY SCREAMING]

1121
01:35:49,460 --> 01:35:50,853
Dad!

1122
01:35:54,682 --> 01:35:55,466
Hang on, Dad!

1123
01:36:03,169 --> 01:36:04,388
Father of Indy,
give me your hand!

1124
01:36:05,432 --> 01:36:06,607
Sallah!

1125
01:36:06,694 --> 01:36:08,174
Get Dad!

1126
01:36:08,261 --> 01:36:09,523
SALLAH:
Give me your hand!

1127
01:36:58,616 --> 01:37:00,487
Junior!

1128
01:37:00,574 --> 01:37:02,098
[RUMBLING]

1129
01:37:02,185 --> 01:37:04,013
Indy!

1130
01:37:12,978 --> 01:37:14,980
Oh, God.

1131
01:37:15,067 --> 01:37:16,895
I've lost him.

1132
01:37:21,291 --> 01:37:23,119
And I never
told him anything.

1133
01:37:26,209 --> 01:37:28,167
I just wasn't
ready, Marcus.

1134
01:37:30,039 --> 01:37:31,867
Five minutes would
have been enough.

1135
01:38:09,382 --> 01:38:11,515
I thought
I'd lost you, boy!

1136
01:38:13,734 --> 01:38:15,127
I thought
you had too, sir.

1137
01:38:28,488 --> 01:38:29,707
Well...

1138
01:38:32,318 --> 01:38:35,017
Well done. Come on.

1139
01:38:38,455 --> 01:38:39,847
Let's go, then.

1140
01:38:43,373 --> 01:38:45,766
Why are you
sitting there resting
when we're so near the end?

1141
01:38:45,853 --> 01:38:47,377
Come on, let's go!

1142
01:39:00,477 --> 01:39:03,610
The Canyon of
the Crescent Moon.

1143
01:40:29,392 --> 01:40:31,524
[MAN SPEAKING GERMAN]

1144
01:41:15,568 --> 01:41:17,048
[BREATHING HEAVILY]

1145
01:41:31,845 --> 01:41:32,455
DONOVAN:
Helmut, another volunteer!

1146
01:41:33,891 --> 01:41:35,632
[MEN CHATTERING]

1147
01:41:48,123 --> 01:41:49,167
[SPEAKING GERMAN]

1148
01:42:02,224 --> 01:42:05,140
I never expected
to see you again.

1149
01:42:05,227 --> 01:42:07,403
I'm like a bad penny,
I always turn up.

1150
01:42:07,490 --> 01:42:09,187
Step back now,
Dr. Schneider.

1151
01:42:09,274 --> 01:42:10,580
Give Dr. Jones
some room.

1152
01:42:12,059 --> 01:42:13,626
He's going to recover
the Grail for us.

1153
01:42:14,758 --> 01:42:15,541
[SCOFFS]

1154
01:42:17,456 --> 01:42:19,850
Impossible?
What do you say, Jones?

1155
01:42:19,937 --> 01:42:21,460
Ready to go down
in history?

1156
01:42:21,547 --> 01:42:24,420
As what?
A Nazi stooge like you?

1157
01:42:24,507 --> 01:42:26,335
The Nazis?

1158
01:42:26,422 --> 01:42:29,512
Is that the limit
of your vision?

1159
01:42:29,599 --> 01:42:32,732
The Nazis want
to write themselves
into the Grail legend,

1160
01:42:32,819 --> 01:42:33,777
take on the world.

1161
01:42:34,908 --> 01:42:36,432
Well, they're welcome.

1162
01:42:38,260 --> 01:42:40,349
But I want
the Grail itself.

1163
01:42:40,436 --> 01:42:42,873
The cup that gives
everlasting life.

1164
01:42:44,875 --> 01:42:48,574
Hitler can have the world,
but he can't take it
with him.

1165
01:42:48,661 --> 01:42:51,403
I'm going to be drinking
my own health when he's
gone the way of the dodo.

1166
01:42:55,755 --> 01:42:58,280
The Grail is mine,

1167
01:42:58,367 --> 01:43:00,543
and you're going
to get it for me.

1168
01:43:00,630 --> 01:43:02,719
Shooting me won't
get you anywhere.

1169
01:43:02,806 --> 01:43:04,460
You know something,
Dr. Jones?

1170
01:43:04,547 --> 01:43:06,288
You're absolutely right.

1171
01:43:06,375 --> 01:43:07,202
[GUNSHOT]

1172
01:43:08,812 --> 01:43:09,682
Dad?

1173
01:43:11,249 --> 01:43:12,163
Dad?

1174
01:43:13,164 --> 01:43:14,296
Junior...

1175
01:43:14,383 --> 01:43:15,732
No!Get back!

1176
01:43:34,446 --> 01:43:36,666
You can't save him
when you're dead!

1177
01:43:36,753 --> 01:43:38,581
The healing power
of the Grail

1178
01:43:38,668 --> 01:43:40,322
is the only thing
that can save your
father now.

1179
01:43:40,409 --> 01:43:43,107
It's time to ask yourself
what you believe.

1180
01:44:19,186 --> 01:44:20,623
INDY: "The Breath of God.

1181
01:44:22,538 --> 01:44:24,540
"Only the penitent man
will pass."

1182
01:44:26,498 --> 01:44:28,326
"The penitent man
will pass."

1183
01:44:47,040 --> 01:44:50,174
The penitent man
will pass.

1184
01:44:50,261 --> 01:44:51,828
The penitent man...

1185
01:44:54,961 --> 01:44:57,616
"Only the penitent man
will pass."

1186
01:44:57,703 --> 01:45:00,315
"Only the penitent man
will pass."

1187
01:45:00,402 --> 01:45:03,927
The penitent man will pass.
The penitent, penitent...

1188
01:45:04,014 --> 01:45:05,711
The penitent man...

1189
01:45:05,798 --> 01:45:08,540
The penitent man...
The penitent...

1190
01:45:08,627 --> 01:45:11,195
The penitent man
is humble before God.

1191
01:45:11,282 --> 01:45:13,023
Penitent. Penitent...

1192
01:45:13,110 --> 01:45:15,068
The penitent man...

1193
01:45:15,155 --> 01:45:17,332
Penitent man is humble,

1194
01:45:18,115 --> 01:45:19,856
kneels before God.

1195
01:45:19,943 --> 01:45:21,466
Kneel!

1196
01:45:29,692 --> 01:45:30,649
I'm through!

1197
01:45:33,348 --> 01:45:34,305
We're through.

1198
01:45:36,916 --> 01:45:38,309
He's all right.

1199
01:45:38,396 --> 01:45:40,659
No.

1200
01:45:40,746 --> 01:45:43,749
The second challenge:
"The Word of God."

1201
01:45:43,836 --> 01:45:46,622
"Only in the footsteps
of God will he proceed."

1202
01:45:50,669 --> 01:45:52,976
The Word of God...

1203
01:45:53,063 --> 01:45:54,064
The Word of...

1204
01:45:58,024 --> 01:46:01,941
Proceed in the footsteps
of the Word.

1205
01:46:02,028 --> 01:46:04,117
"The Word of God..." BRODY: No, Henry.
Try not to talk.

1206
01:46:11,734 --> 01:46:13,083
The name of God...

1207
01:46:15,172 --> 01:46:17,304
The name of God...

1208
01:46:19,306 --> 01:46:22,397
Jehovah.

1209
01:46:22,484 --> 01:46:27,010
But in the Latin alphabet,
Jehovah begins with an "I."

1210
01:46:27,880 --> 01:46:28,881
J...

1211
01:46:35,322 --> 01:46:36,280
Oh, dear.

1212
01:46:40,806 --> 01:46:43,026
Idiot!

1213
01:46:43,113 --> 01:46:44,854
In Latin, Jehovah
starts with an "I."

1214
01:46:47,944 --> 01:46:49,206
"I."

1215
01:46:50,120 --> 01:46:50,903
"E."

1216
01:46:52,862 --> 01:46:53,602
"H."

1217
01:46:54,472 --> 01:46:55,691
"O."

1218
01:46:57,040 --> 01:46:58,650
"V."

1219
01:46:58,737 --> 01:46:59,912
"A."

1220
01:47:22,848 --> 01:47:24,067
"The Path of God."

1221
01:47:28,506 --> 01:47:29,768
"Only in the leap
from the lion's head

1222
01:47:31,204 --> 01:47:33,076
"will he prove his worth."

1223
01:47:37,602 --> 01:47:39,909
Impossible,
nobody can jump this.

1224
01:47:41,214 --> 01:47:42,041
[MOANS]

1225
01:47:43,086 --> 01:47:43,739
BRODY: Indy!

1226
01:47:44,740 --> 01:47:46,306
Indy, you must hurry!

1227
01:47:46,393 --> 01:47:47,699
Come quickly!

1228
01:47:54,097 --> 01:47:56,578
It's a leap of faith.

1229
01:47:56,665 --> 01:47:58,275
Oh, geez...

1230
01:47:58,362 --> 01:48:00,495
You must believe, boy.

1231
01:48:00,582 --> 01:48:02,627
You must believe.

1232
01:48:22,212 --> 01:48:23,082
[GASPS]

1233
01:49:49,778 --> 01:49:52,737
I knew you'd come,

1234
01:49:52,824 --> 01:49:54,260
but my strength
has left me.

1235
01:49:55,435 --> 01:49:57,176
Who are you?

1236
01:49:57,263 --> 01:49:59,483
The last of
three brothers

1237
01:49:59,570 --> 01:50:02,312
who swore an oath
to find the Grail

1238
01:50:02,399 --> 01:50:03,922
and to guard it.

1239
01:50:05,315 --> 01:50:07,230
That was 700 years ago.

1240
01:50:07,317 --> 01:50:09,232
A long time to wait.

1241
01:50:12,017 --> 01:50:14,803
You're strangely dressed,
for a knight.

1242
01:50:16,021 --> 01:50:17,457
I'm not exactly...

1243
01:50:18,981 --> 01:50:19,938
A knight?
What do you mean?

1244
01:50:20,939 --> 01:50:22,985
I was chosen

1245
01:50:23,072 --> 01:50:26,553
because I was the bravest,
the most worthy.

1246
01:50:26,641 --> 01:50:29,948
The honor was mine
until another came

1247
01:50:30,035 --> 01:50:32,603
to challenge me
to single combat.

1248
01:50:34,387 --> 01:50:39,654
I pass it to you
who vanquished me.

1249
01:50:43,832 --> 01:50:45,572
Listen, I don't have
time to explain, but...

1250
01:51:03,068 --> 01:51:03,982
Which one is it?

1251
01:51:04,809 --> 01:51:06,985
You must choose,

1252
01:51:07,072 --> 01:51:09,684
but choose wisely.

1253
01:51:09,771 --> 01:51:12,687
For as the true Grail
will bring you life,

1254
01:51:12,774 --> 01:51:14,210
the false Grail

1255
01:51:14,297 --> 01:51:15,733
will take it from you.

1256
01:51:22,392 --> 01:51:24,176
DONOVAN:
I'm not a historian.

1257
01:51:24,263 --> 01:51:26,004
I have no idea
what it looks like.

1258
01:51:26,091 --> 01:51:28,398
Which one is it?

1259
01:51:28,485 --> 01:51:29,791
Let me choose.

1260
01:51:31,140 --> 01:51:32,707
Thank you, Doctor.

1261
01:51:45,937 --> 01:51:47,634
Oh, yes.

1262
01:51:53,945 --> 01:51:56,643
It's more beautiful
than I'd ever imagined.

1263
01:52:08,830 --> 01:52:11,310
This certainly is the cup
of the King of Kings.

1264
01:52:18,100 --> 01:52:19,754
Eternal life.

1265
01:52:44,648 --> 01:52:45,518
[GASPS]

1266
01:52:55,267 --> 01:52:57,139
What is happening to me?

1267
01:53:00,882 --> 01:53:02,318
[SCREAMING]

1268
01:53:02,405 --> 01:53:03,580
DONOVAN: Tell me,
what is happening?

1269
01:53:23,513 --> 01:53:24,949
[WIND BLOWING]

1270
01:53:27,212 --> 01:53:29,345
He chose

1271
01:53:29,432 --> 01:53:30,912
poorly.

1272
01:53:41,531 --> 01:53:43,881
ELSA: It would not
be made out of gold.

1273
01:53:43,968 --> 01:53:45,448
That's the cup
of a carpenter.

1274
01:53:56,198 --> 01:53:57,503
There's only one
way to find out.

1275
01:54:12,562 --> 01:54:15,870
You have chosen wisely.

1276
01:54:17,697 --> 01:54:22,398
But the Grail cannot pass
beyond the Great Seal.

1277
01:54:22,485 --> 01:54:26,489
That is the boundary
and the price of
immortality.

1278
01:54:53,255 --> 01:54:53,995
[SIZZLES]

1279
01:54:54,996 --> 01:54:55,866
[MOANS]

1280
01:55:49,876 --> 01:55:50,790
[MAN SPEAKING
FOREIGN LANGUAGE]

1281
01:55:53,402 --> 01:55:55,752
Drop your guns. Please.

1282
01:55:59,799 --> 01:56:00,887
Dad, come on,
get to your feet.

1283
01:56:16,033 --> 01:56:17,426
We have got it.
Come on!

1284
01:56:19,515 --> 01:56:22,431
Elsa!
Elsa, don't move!

1285
01:56:22,518 --> 01:56:24,607
It's ours, Indy,
yours and mine.

1286
01:56:24,694 --> 01:56:27,088
INDY: Elsa,
don't cross the Seal.

1287
01:56:27,175 --> 01:56:29,525
The knight warned us
not to take the Grail
from here.

1288
01:56:29,612 --> 01:56:30,613
[RUMBLING]

1289
01:56:55,986 --> 01:56:56,987
HENRY: Junior!

1290
01:56:57,074 --> 01:56:58,597
Junior!

1291
01:57:01,252 --> 01:57:02,210
Elsa...

1292
01:57:03,907 --> 01:57:05,300
Elsa, don't. Elsa...

1293
01:57:05,952 --> 01:57:08,259
Elsa...

1294
01:57:08,346 --> 01:57:10,305
Give me your
other hand, honey.
I can't hold you!

1295
01:57:10,392 --> 01:57:12,698
I can reach it.
I can reach it.

1296
01:57:12,785 --> 01:57:14,570
Elsa, give me your hand.
Give me your other hand!

1297
01:57:17,529 --> 01:57:18,922
Elsa!

1298
01:57:19,009 --> 01:57:20,489
[SCREAMING]

1299
01:57:29,454 --> 01:57:30,803
Junior, give me
your other hand!

1300
01:57:30,890 --> 01:57:32,849
I can't hold on.

1301
01:57:32,936 --> 01:57:34,285
I can get it.

1302
01:57:34,372 --> 01:57:36,113
I can almost
reach it, Dad.

1303
01:57:36,896 --> 01:57:38,376
Indiana...

1304
01:57:39,899 --> 01:57:40,813
Indiana...

1305
01:57:44,426 --> 01:57:46,384
Let it go.

1306
01:58:05,925 --> 01:58:07,231
Dad...

1307
01:58:14,151 --> 01:58:15,674
Please, Dad.

1308
01:58:47,880 --> 01:58:50,796
Elsa never really
believed in the Grail.

1309
01:58:50,883 --> 01:58:52,972
She thought
she'd found a prize.

1310
01:58:59,675 --> 01:59:01,677
What did you find, Dad?

1311
01:59:01,764 --> 01:59:03,113
Me?

1312
01:59:05,942 --> 01:59:07,857
Illumination.

1313
01:59:17,780 --> 01:59:18,215
And what did you find,
Junior?

1314
01:59:19,216 --> 01:59:21,784
"Junior"? Dad...

1315
01:59:21,871 --> 01:59:24,134
Please, what does
it always mean, this...
This "Junior"?

1316
01:59:24,221 --> 01:59:26,702
That's his name:
Henry Jones, Junior.

1317
01:59:26,789 --> 01:59:28,660
I like Indiana.

1318
01:59:28,747 --> 01:59:30,836
We named the dog Indiana.

1319
01:59:30,923 --> 01:59:32,795
May we go home now, please?

1320
01:59:32,882 --> 01:59:33,839
The dog?

1321
01:59:35,319 --> 01:59:37,016
You are named
after the dog?

1322
01:59:40,411 --> 01:59:43,109
I've got a lot
of fond memories
of that dog.

1323
01:59:44,894 --> 01:59:45,895
INDY: Ready?

1324
01:59:45,982 --> 01:59:47,723
Ready.

1325
01:59:47,810 --> 01:59:50,291
Indy! Henry! Follow me.
I know the way!

1326
01:59:56,732 --> 01:59:58,299
Got lost in his
own museum, huh?

1327
01:59:58,386 --> 01:59:59,256
Uh-huh.

1328
02:00:00,257 --> 02:00:02,651
After you, Junior.

1329
02:00:02,738 --> 02:00:04,130
Yes, sir.

